'''Robert Lewandowski''' ({{IPA|pl|ˈrɔbɛrt lɛvanˈdɔfskʲi|-|Pl-Robert Lewandowski.ogg}}; born 21 August 1988) is a Polish professional [[Association football|footballer]] who plays as a [[Striker (association football)|striker]] for [[Major League Soccer]] club [[Chicago Fire FC|Chicago Fire]] and [[Captain (association football)|captains]] the [[Poland national football team|Poland national team]]. Widely regarded as one of the greatest strikers of all time, he is one of only five players to have scored 100 goals with three different clubs,{{refn|group=note|The other four players are [[Isidro Lángara]], [[Romário]], [[Neymar]] and [[Cristiano Ronaldo]].}} ranks third for the all-time [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics#All-time top scorers|top goalscorers]] in the [[UEFA Champions League]] with 109 goals,<ref>{{cite web |title=All-time Player &#124; Top goalscorers Stats &#124; UEFA Champions League |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/rankings/players/goals_scored/ |access-date=3 August 2025 |publisher=UEFA |archive-date=10 October 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251010221537/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/rankings/players/goals_scored/ |url-status=live }}</ref> and ranks third for the [[List of men's footballers with 50 or more international goals|all-time European men's top goalscorers]] in international football (92). He has scored over [[List of footballers with 500 or more goals|750 senior career goals]] for club and country. In addition, he is also one of a select number of players who have [[List of men's footballers with 1,000 or more official appearances|amassed over 1,100 career appearances]] (the first Pole to achieve the feat, since the time matches are recorded).

Beginning his career in the third and second tiers of Polish football with [[Znicz Pruszków]], Lewandowski moved to top-flight [[Lech Poznań]], helping the team win the [[2009–10 Ekstraklasa]]. In 2010, he transferred to [[Borussia Dortmund]], where he won two consecutive [[Meisterschale|Bundesliga titles]] and the [[2013–14 Bundesliga#Top scorers|league's top goalscorer]] award. In 2013, he also featured with Dortmund in the [[2013 UEFA Champions League final]]. Ahead of the [[2014–15 Bundesliga|2014–15 season]], Lewandowski joined Dortmund's domestic rivals, [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]], on a [[Free transfer (association football)|free transfer]]. In Munich, he won the Bundesliga title in all of his eight seasons at the club and was integral in their Champions League win in [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League|2019–20]] as part of a [[Treble (association football)|treble]]. Lewandowski was widely expected to win the 2020 [[Ballon d'Or]], but it was not awarded due to the [[Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on association football|impact of COVID-19]]. In 2022, he moved to [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]], where he won three [[La Liga]] titles, the [[Copa del Rey]], and the [[Pichichi Trophy]]; his Pichichi Trophy win made him the joint-record holder for most top scorer awards in [[Big Five (association football)|Europe's top five leagues]] with eight.<ref name=":4">{{cite web |last=Gajdek |first=Michał |date=17 July 2023 |title=Sport: Rekordowe wyzwanie Roberta Lewandowskiego |url=https://www.fcbarca.com/111409-sport-rekordowe-wyzwanie-roberta-lewandowskiego.html |access-date=23 January 2024 |website=FCBarca |language=pl |archive-date=23 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240123044930/https://www.fcbarca.com/111409-sport-rekordowe-wyzwanie-roberta-lewandowskiego.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Following his departure from Barcelona at the end of the [[2025–26 FC Barcelona season|2025–26 season]], Lewandowski joined Major League Soccer club Chicago Fire in June 2026. 

A full international for Poland since 2008, Lewandowski has earned 170 caps, and was a member of their team at the [[UEFA European Championship]] in [[UEFA Euro 2012|2012]], [[UEFA Euro 2016|2016]], [[UEFA Euro 2020|2020]], and [[UEFA Euro 2024|2024]], and the [[FIFA World Cup]] in [[2018 FIFA World Cup|2018]] and [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022]]. Having captained the national team since 2014, he briefly retired in 2025, before returning and being reinstated as captain later the same year. With 92 international goals, he is the all-time top scorer for Poland.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro/history/news/0253-0d81f47dfe05-e92503acfb7d-1000--europe-s-top-international-scorers/ |title=Europe's top international scorers: Cristiano Ronaldo out in front |publisher=UEFA |date=13 June 2022 |access-date=14 July 2022 |archive-date=14 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220714010342/https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro/history/news/0253-0d81f47dfe05-e92503acfb7d-1000--europe-s-top-international-scorers/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Lewandowski has been named the [[Piłka Nożna magazine plebiscite|Polish Footballer of the Year]] a record thirteen times and the [[Polish Sports Personality of the Year]] three times.

Lewandowski won the [[The Best FIFA Men's Player|Best FIFA Men's Player Award]] in 2020 and 2021. Further awards include the [[UEFA Men's Player of the Year Award]] in 2020, the [[IFFHS World's Best Player]] in 2020 and 2021, the [[European Golden Shoe]] for the 2020–21 and 2021–22 seasons, and the [[Gerd Müller Trophy]] in 2021 and 2022. He was runner-up for the Ballon d'Or in [[2021 Ballon d'Or|2021]]. He is one of the most successful players in [[Bundesliga]] and Bayern history, being named the [[Vereinigung der Vertragsfußballspieler#Awards|VDV Player of the Season (Silver Boot)]] a record five times and winning the [[List of Bundesliga top scorers by season|Bundesliga Top Scorer Award]] in a joint-record seven seasons, among other records.

==Club career==

===Early career===
{{Quote box|width=32%|align=left|quote="When I was six, I remember [[Roberto Baggio]] at the 1994 World Cup. When I was between 10 and 14, [[Alessandro Del Piero]] was the best player for me. Then my idol was [[Thierry Henry]]. He was amazing – it wasn't just how he scored the goals but what he did for the team."|source=—Lewandowski on his childhood role models<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/news/robert-lewandowski-names-arsenal-player-who-was-his-idol |title=Robert Lewandowski names the Arsenal player who was his idol |last=Mackenzie |first=Alasdair |date=7 March 2019 |website=[[FourFourTwo]] |access-date=19 August 2020 |archive-date=23 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210523203810/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/news/robert-lewandowski-names-arsenal-player-who-was-his-idol |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

Lewandowski was born in [[Warsaw]]<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.eurosport.co.uk/football/robert-lewandowski_prs141648/person.shtml |title=Robert Lewandowski |website=[[Eurosport]] |access-date=21 March 2020 |archive-date=1 September 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190901011429/https://www.eurosport.co.uk/football/robert-lewandowski_prs141648/person.shtml |url-status=live }}</ref> and grew up in [[Leszno, Warsaw West County|Leszno]], [[Warsaw West County]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Gontarczyk |first=Karolina |date=26 April 2019 |title=Otwarte spotkanie z Robertem Lewandowskim w Lesznie |trans-title=Open meeting with Robert Lewandowski in Leszno |url=http://zachodniemazowsze.info/2019/04/otwarte-spotkanie-robertem-lewandowskim-lesznie/ |work=Zachodnie Mazowsze |access-date=21 March 2020 |archive-date=21 March 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200321164857/http://zachodniemazowsze.info/2019/04/otwarte-spotkanie-robertem-lewandowskim-lesznie/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He took his first steps in football as an unregistered player for the local club, Partyzant Leszno.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hurkowski |first1=Rafał |last2=Murawski |first2=Robert |date=25 August 2015 |title=Śladami Lewandowskiego: Partyzant Leszno |trans-title=Lewandowski's Steps: Partyzant Leszno |url=https://www.polsatsport.pl/wiadomosc/2015-08-25/sladami-lewandowskiego-partyzant-leszno/ |language=pl |work=[[Polsat Sport]] |location=Warsaw |publisher=[[Cyfrowy Polsat]] |access-date=5 June 2019 |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806202811/https://www.polsatsport.pl/wiadomosc/2015-08-25/sladami-lewandowskiego-partyzant-leszno/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In 1997, he joined MKS Varsovia Warsaw, where as a teen he played for seven years.<ref name="bajka">{{cite news |last=Cegliński |first=Łukasz |date=11 September 2008 |title=Bajka o Robercie Lewandowskim |trans-title=The Tale of Robert Lewandowski |url=http://www.sport.pl/celebrities/1,96810,5686861,Bajka_o_Robercie_Lewandowskim.html |language=pl |work=[[Agora (company)#Gazeta.pl|Sport.pl]] |location=Warsaw |publisher=[[Agora (company)|Agora]] |access-date=1 May 2012 |archive-date=19 April 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120419004234/http://www.sport.pl/celebrities/1,96810,5686861,Bajka_o_Robercie_Lewandowskim.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The following year he moved to [[III liga|fourth tier]] side Delta Warsaw, where he finally managed to play in the first team, scoring four goals at the end of the season.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kuna |first=Tomasz |title=.: liga polska |url=http://www.90minut.pl/strzelcy.php?id=1357 |access-date=19 November 2012 |publisher=90minut.pl |language=pl |archive-date=25 October 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121025020137/http://www.90minut.pl/strzelcy.php?id=1357 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In {{ill|2006–07 III liga|lt=2006–07|pl|III liga polska w piłce nożnej (2006/2007)}}, Lewandowski was the [[II liga|Polish third division]]'s top goalscorer with 15 goals, helping [[Znicz Pruszków]] win the promotion.<ref name="krol">{{Cite news |last=Leśniowski |first=Piotr |date=26 May 2008 |title="Kolejorz" walczy o króla |language=pl |work=[[Gazeta Wyborcza]] |url=http://www.archiwum.wyborcza.pl/Archiwum/1,0,5158528,20080527PO-DLO_D,Kolejorz_walczy_o_krola,.html |access-date=1 December 2013 |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806225144/http://www.archiwum.wyborcza.pl/Archiwum/1,0,5158528,20080527PO-DLO_D,Kolejorz_walczy_o_krola,.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[2007–08 II liga|next season]], he was the [[2007–08 II liga#Top goalscorers|top scorer]] in the [[I liga|Polish second highest division]] with 21 goals.<ref name=krol/>

===Lech Poznań===
[[File:LewandowskiR.jpg|thumb|upright|right|Lewandowski playing for [[Lech Poznań]] in 2009]]
In June 2008, [[Lech Poznań]] signed Lewandowski from Znicz for 1.5&nbsp;million [[Polish złoty|PLN]]. (398,670 [[Dollar|Dollars]], or 350,055 [[Euro|Euros]])<ref name="bajka" /><ref name="official">{{Cite news |date=18 June 2008 |title=Robert Lewandowski piłkarzem Kolejorza |language=pl |work=[[Lech Poznań]] |url=http://www.lechpoznan.pl/art,9498,robert_lewandowski_pilkarzem_kolejorza_.htm |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080917233558/http://www.lechpoznan.pl/art%2C9498%2Crobert_lewandowski_pilkarzem_kolejorza_.htm |archive-date=17 September 2008}}</ref> Earlier that month, Lewandowski's agent [[Cezary Kucharski]] offered him to his former team [[Sporting Gijón]], which [[2008–09 La Liga|had been promoted]] to the [[La Liga]], Spain's first division, after ten years in the [[Segunda División]]. However, Gijón rejected him.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 April 2013 |title=Lewandowski was offered to Preciado's Sporting |url=http://www.resultados-futbol.com/noticia/lewandowski-fue-ofrecido-al-sporting-preciado |access-date=14 September 2013 |website=[[Terra Networks|Terra]] |archive-date=27 September 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130927182008/http://www.resultados-futbol.com/noticia/lewandowski-fue-ofrecido-al-sporting-preciado |url-status=live }}</ref>

He debuted for Lech on 17 July 2008 as a substitute in the [[2008–09 UEFA Cup qualifying rounds#First qualifying round|first qualifying first leg match]] of the [[2008–09 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]] versus [[Khazar Lankaran FK|Khazar Lankaran]] from [[Azerbaijan]], in which he scored the only goal of the evening in the 75th minute at the [[Tofiq Bahramov Republican Stadium]]. During his [[Ekstraklasa]] debut in the first game of the season, in a match against [[GKS Bełchatów]], he scored a heel flick goal just four minutes after coming into the game late second half. In [[2008–09 Ekstraklasa|his first season]] in the Polish top division, he was second in the goal-scoring charts. Lewandowski finished the season with 18 goals in 42 matches.<ref name="ClubMatches">{{Cite web |title=Robert Lewandowski » Club matches |url=https://www.worldfootball.net/person/pe17534/robert-lewandowski/club-matches/ |access-date=6 February 2018 |publisher=World Football |archive-date=29 July 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190729190423/https://www.worldfootball.net/player_summary/robert-lewandowski/2/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He also scored in a 1–1 away draw against [[Wisła Kraków]] in the [[2009 Polish Super Cup]] on 27 July, and converted his attempt in the won [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]]. The next season, he became the [[2009–10 Ekstraklasa#Top goalscorers|top scorer]] with 18 goals and helped his team win the [[2009–10 Ekstraklasa|2009–10 championship]].<ref name="ClubMatches"/>

English coach, [[Sam Allardyce]], said that Lewandowski was about to join [[Premier League]] club [[Blackburn Rovers F.C.|Blackburn Rovers]] in 2010, but the volcanic ash clouds caused by the [[2010 eruptions of Eyjafjallajökull]] which suspended all flights in and out of the UK, in addition to other financial worries, prevented the potential transfer.<ref>{{Cite web |date=26 April 2013 |title=Robert Lewandowski: Sam Allardyce regrets missed signing |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/22305776 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=27 June 2020 |archive-date=23 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200723093427/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/22305776 |url-status=live }}</ref> Moreover, Lewandowski was also about to join Italian club [[Genoa C.F.C.|Genoa]], before president [[Enrico Preziosi]] decided to cancel the transfer.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 April 2020 |title=Lewandowski 'deal was done' for Genoa before president 'pulled the plug' |url=https://www.goal.com/en-om/news/lewandowski-deal-was-done-for-genoa/10mwwby95gq521hrayoljtafbm |website=Goal.com |access-date=16 December 2020 |archive-date=23 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210523203810/https://www.goal.com/en-om/news/lewandowski-deal-was-done-for-genoa/10mwwby95gq521hrayoljtafbm |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Borussia Dortmund===

====2010–2012: League and cup double====
[[File:Robert Lewandowski 2010.jpg|thumb|upright|left|Lewandowski at [[Borussia Dortmund]] in 2010]]
Following press speculation that Lewandowski might move to one of a number of clubs,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Adamoli |first=Gessi |date=14 April 2010 |title=Accordo con Lewandowski è arrivato il bomber polacco |language=it |work=[[la Repubblica]] |url=http://ricerca.repubblica.it/repubblica/archivio/repubblica/2010/04/14/accordo-con-lewandowski-arrivato-il-bomber-polacco.html |access-date=19 May 2010 |archive-date=18 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190418163348/https://ricerca.repubblica.it/repubblica/archivio/repubblica/2010/04/14/accordo-con-lewandowski-arrivato-il-bomber-polacco.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=17 April 2010 |title=Blackburn target Lewandowski cancels trip to UK |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=772358&cc=5901 |access-date=19 May 2010 |publisher=[[ESPN FC]] |archive-date=24 October 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121024134945/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=772358&cc=5901 |url-status=dead }}</ref> he joined [[Bundesliga]] club [[Borussia Dortmund]] in June 2010, signing a four-year contract with the German club<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 June 2010 |title=Dortmund macht Lewandowski-Transfer perfekt |url=http://www.focus.de/sport/fussball/bundesliga1/bundesliga-dortmund-macht-lewandowski-transfer-perfekt_aid_518537.html |access-date=11 June 2010 |website=Focus |language=de |archive-date=27 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180627212758/https://www.focus.de/sport/fussball/bundesliga1/bundesliga-dortmund-macht-lewandowski-transfer-perfekt_aid_518537.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> for a fee reported to be worth around [[Euro|€]]4.5&nbsp;million.<ref>{{Cite web |date=18 November 2010 |title=Borussia Dortmund striker Robert Lewandowski hits double as Poland beat Ivory Coast (video) |url=http://www.imscouting.com/global-news-article/Borussia-Dortmund-striker-Robert-Lewandowski-hits-double-as-Poland-beat-Ivory-Coast-/12139/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120330155229/http://www.imscouting.com/global-news-article/Borussia-Dortmund-striker-Robert-Lewandowski-hits-double-as-Poland-beat-Ivory-Coast-/12139/ |archive-date=30 March 2012 |access-date=1 May 2012 |publisher=imscouting.com}}</ref> On 19 September, he scored his first goal in the Bundesliga to make it 3–0 in the [[Revierderby]] against [[FC Schalke 04|Schalke 04]]; the game ended 3–1.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 September 2010 |title=Schalke 04 1–3 Borussia Dortmund |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/match?id=297229&cc=5901 |access-date=1 December 2013 |publisher=ESPN FC |archive-date=9 November 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121109220519/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/match?id=297229 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

In the [[2011–12 Bundesliga|2011–12 Bundesliga campaign]], Lewandowski profited from an injury to [[Lucas Barrios]] and he was elevated to an ever-present position in the starting XI until the winter break. The striker responded by finding the net two times in Dortmund's 3–0 [[2011–12 DFB-Pokal#First round|DFB-Pokal first round]] victory over [[SV Sandhausen|Sandhausen]].<ref>2011–12 DFB-Pokal</ref> Lewandowski opened his league account in a 2–0 win over [[1. FC Nürnberg|Nürnberg]] on 20 August 2011 by providing the finishing touch from a [[Mario Götze]] cross.<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 August 2011 |title=Borussia Dortmund 2 – 0 Nurnberg |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=321020&cc=5901 |access-date=17 May 2012 |publisher=ESPN FC |archive-date=22 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190122044319/http://www.espn.com/soccer/report?id=321020&cc=5901 |url-status=dead }}</ref> On 1 October, Lewandowski netted a [[hat-trick]] and provided an [[Assist (association football)|assist]] in the club's 4–0 victory over [[FC Augsburg|Augsburg]], following a disappointing 0–3 loss to [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] in the [[2011–12 UEFA Champions League group stage#Group F|UEFA Champions League group stage]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 October 2011 |title=Borussia Dortmund 4 – 0 FC Augsburg |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/match/_/gameId/320971/fc-augsburg-borussia-dortmund |access-date=1 September 2026 |publisher=ESPN |archive-date=3 September 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260903024205/https://www.espn.com/soccer/match/_/gameId/320971/fc-augsburg-borussia-dortmund |url-status=live }}</ref> He later scored his first Champions League goal in a 1–3 [[Karaiskakis Stadium|away]] defeat to [[Olympiacos F.C.|Olympiacos]] on 19 October.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 February 2019 |title=All of Robert Lewandowski's #UCL goals |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/video/024e-0e68e5fafae8-e63ef51fb17d-1000--all-of-robert-lewandowski-s-ucl-goals/ |publisher=UEFA |access-date=4 October 2020 |archive-date=21 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210521073303/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/video/024e-0e68e5fafae8-e63ef51fb17d-1000--all-of-robert-lewandowski-s-ucl-goals/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Dortmund climbed into second place in the Bundesliga with a comfortable 5–0 victory over [[1. FC Köln|Köln]] on 22 October, with Lewandowski finding the net either side of half-time.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 October 2011 |title=Borussia Dortmund 5–0 Koln: Five-star champions move second in Bundesliga table |url=http://www.goal.com/en-us/match/60749/borussia-dortmund-vs-fc-k%C3%B6ln/report |access-date=17 May 2012 |website=Goal |publisher=Perform Group |location=Leeds |archive-date=21 October 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131021063906/http://www.goal.com/en-us/match/60749/borussia-dortmund-vs-fc-k%C3%B6ln/report |url-status=live }}</ref> Dortmund travelled to [[SC Freiburg|Freiburg]] on 17 December and Lewandowski struck twice and provided an assist for [[Kevin Großkreutz]], as Dortmund eased to a 4–1 triumph, scoring his first hat-trick in Bundesliga.<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 December 2011 |title=Report: SC Freiburg 1 – 4 Borussia Dortmund |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/match/_/gameId/320891/borussia-dortmund-sc-freiburg |access-date=1 September 2026 |publisher=ESPN FC |archive-date=2 September 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260902211039/https://www.espn.com/soccer/match/_/gameId/320891/borussia-dortmund-sc-freiburg |url-status=live }}</ref> Due to his strong performances, he was named [[Piłka Nożna magazine plebiscite|Footballer of the Year in Poland]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=12 December 2011 |title=Lewandowski ist Polens Bester |url=https://www.kicker.de/lewandowski-ist-polens-bester-561944/artikel |access-date=10 November 2012 |website=[[Kicker (magazine)|Kicker]] |language=de |archive-date=21 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190121064735/http://www.kicker.de/news/fussball/intligen/startseite/561944/artikel_lewandowski-ist-polens-bester.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Following the winter break, on 22 January 2012, Dortmund thrashed [[Hamburger SV|Hamburg]] 5–1 to move level on points with leaders [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]]; Lewandowski netted twice and added an assist for [[Jakub Błaszczykowski]] in the rout.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 January 2012 |title=Report: Hamburg SV 1 – 5 Borussia Dortmund |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=320883&cc=5901 |access-date=17 May 2012 |publisher=ESPN FC |archive-date=26 January 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120126150331/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=320883 |url-status=dead }}</ref> He scored in a 1–0 home win over Bayern Munich on 11 April.<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 April 2012 |title=Borussia Dortmund see off Bayern Munich to close on successive title |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2012/apr/11/borussia-dortmund-bayern-munich-match-report |access-date=17 May 2012 |website=The Guardian |archive-date=5 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305200741/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2012/apr/11/borussia-dortmund-bayern-munich-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> The result gave Dortmund a six-point cushion over their title rivals with only four games left to play.<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 April 2012 |title=Borussia Dortmund 1 – 0 Bayern Munich |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=320773&cc=5901 |access-date=17 May 2012 |publisher=ESPN FC |archive-date=17 April 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120417181017/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=320773 |url-status=dead }}</ref> On 21 April, Lewandowski provided the assist for [[Shinji Kagawa]]'s 59th-minute goal as Dortmund won 2–0 over [[Borussia Mönchengladbach]] to seal their second straight title.<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 April 2012 |title=Borussia Dortmund 2–0 Borussia Monchengladbach: Perisic and Kagawa trigger celebrations as Jurgen Klopp's men retain Bundesliga crown |url=http://www.goal.com/en-us/match/61119/borussia-dortmund-vs-m%C3%B6nchengladbach/report |access-date=17 May 2012 |website=Goal |publisher=Perform Group |location=Leeds |archive-date=18 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190418152052/https://www.goal.com/en-us/match/61119/borussia-dortmund-vs-m%C3%B6nchengladbach/report |url-status=live }}</ref> In the final Bundesliga game of the campaign, Lewandowski scored two first-half goals as Dortmund beat Freiburg 4–0 and celebrated lifting the title.<ref>{{Cite web |date=5 May 2012 |title=Borussia Dortmund 4 – 0 SC Freiburg |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/match_facts/0,,11065_3412576,00.html |access-date=17 May 2012 |publisher=Sky Sports |archive-date=5 July 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120705071316/http://www.skysports.com/football/match_facts/0,,11065_3412576,00.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Lewandowski finished the year as the third top goal scorer with 22 goals, none from the penalty spot, and six assists.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Goals & Assists |url=http://www.bundesliga.de/en/liga/scorer/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120508204855/http://www.bundesliga.de/en/liga/scorer/ |archive-date=8 May 2012 |access-date=12 May 2012 |publisher=bundesliga.de}}</ref>

On 12 May, in the final game of the [[2011–12 Borussia Dortmund season|season for Dortmund]], he scored a hat-trick in the [[2012 DFB-Pokal Final|DFB-Pokal Final]], a 5–2 win over Bayern Munich, to earn the club its first domestic [[Double (association football)|double]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=12 May 2012 |title=Dortmund complete double |url=http://www1.skysports.com/football/news/12040/7752618/Dortmund-complete-double |access-date=17 May 2012 |publisher=Sky Sports |archive-date=17 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200217183534/https://www.skysports.com/football/news/12040/7752618/dortmund-complete-double |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=12 May 2012 |title=Borussia Dortmund rout Bayern Munich to claim double |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story/_/id/1069150/borussia-dortmund-rout-bayern-munich-to-claim-historic-double?cc=5901 |access-date=17 May 2012 |publisher=ESPN FC |archive-date=15 May 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515140955/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story/_/id/1069150/borussia-dortmund-rout-bayern-munich-to-claim-historic-double?cc=5901 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Lewandowski finished as the [[2011–12 DFB-Pokal#Top goalscorers|DFB-Pokal's top goalscorer]], with seven goals from six games.<ref>{{Cite web |title=DFB-Pokal – Torjäger |trans-title=DFB Cup – Goalscorer |url=https://www.kicker.de/dfb-pokal/torjaeger/2011-12/6 |access-date=17 May 2012 |website=[[Kicker (magazine)|Kicker]] |language=de |archive-date=21 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190121064658/http://www.kicker.de/news/fussball/dfbpokal/spielrunde/dfb-pokal/2011-12/torjaeger-der-saison.html.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

====2012–2014: Champions League runner-up and league top goalscorer====
[[File:Robert Lewandowski 2013 in Wilhelmshaven.jpeg|thumb|right|upright|Lewandowski playing for [[Borussia Dortmund]] in 2013]]

On 12 August 2012, Lewandowski began the [[2012-13 Borussia Dortmund season|2012–13 season]] by scoring in the 1–2 [[2012 DFL-Supercup]] defeat to Bayern Munich.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Daniel |first=Chris Punnakkattu |date=12 August 2012 |title=DFL Supercup 2012: FC Bayern Munich 2–1 Borussia Dortmund |work=The Blog » CPD Football by Chris Punnakkattu Daniel |url=https://blog.cpdfootball.de/2012/08/12/dfl-supercup-2012-fc-bayern-munich-v-borussia-dortmund/ |access-date=29 April 2021 |publisher=CPD Football |archive-date=29 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210429113018/https://blog.cpdfootball.de/2012/08/12/dfl-supercup-2012-fc-bayern-munich-v-borussia-dortmund/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He made his first appearance of the [[2012–13 Bundesliga]] campaign in Dortmund's 2–1 victory over [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] on the opening day of the season.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 August 2012 |title=Borussia Dortmund 2–1 Werder Bremen |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/match?id=346403&cc=5901 |access-date=24 November 2012 |publisher=ESPN FC |archive-date=29 August 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120829132410/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/match?id=346403 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

He netted his first goal in a 3–0 victory over [[Bayer 04 Leverkusen|Bayer Leverkusen]] on 15 September 2012, extending Dortmund's run to 31 games unbeaten and moved the club into third in the Bundesliga.<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 September 2012 |title=Dortmund 3–0 Leverkusen |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=346378&cc=5901 |access-date=24 November 2012 |publisher=ESPN FC |archive-date=22 September 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120922200130/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=346378 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Three days later, in the club's first [[2012–13 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] game of the season, Lewandowski scored an 87th-minute winner to defeat [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]], 1–0.<ref>{{Cite web |last=James |first=Andy |date=18 September 2012 |title=Dortmund leave it late to defeat Ajax |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0201-0e7f16150cec-92561b6fb805-1000--dortmund-leave-it-late-to-defeat-ajax/ |access-date=25 April 2013 |publisher=UEFA |archive-date=23 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190123121506/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/matches/round=2000347/match=2009502/postmatch/report/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He set club's new record of the longest scoring streak, having scored in 12 consecutive league games, surpassing [[Friedhelm Konietzka]]'s record from [[1964–65 Bundesliga|1964–65 season]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 March 2013 |title=BVB-Torjäger Lewandowski überholt Konietzka |trans-title=BVB striker Lewandowski overtakes Konietzka |url=https://www.ruhrnachrichten.de/bvb/bvb-torjaeger-lewandowski-ueberholt-konietzka-538280.html |access-date=28 April 2013 |publisher=ruhrnachrichten.de |language=de |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121212249/https://www.ruhrnachrichten.de/sport/bvb/19-Saisontreffer-BVB-Torjaeger-Lewandowski-ueberholt-Konietzka;art11635,1942266 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

On 9 February 2013, he opened the scoring in a home match against Hamburg, but was sent off in the 31st minute for a foul on [[Per Ciljan Skjelbred]] and Dortmund lost 4–1.<ref>{{cite web |last=Honigstein |first=Raphael |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2013/feb/11/dortmund-defeat-bundesliga-bayern-raphael-honigstein |title=Dortmund defeat as good as hands the Bundesliga title to Bayern |publisher=The Guardian |date=11 February 2013 }}</ref> According to Borussia Dortmund director [[Michael Zorc]], speaking in February 2013, Lewandowski would not be renewing his contract with the club, and would leave either in the summer of 2013 or after the 2013–14 season.<ref>{{Cite news |date=28 February 2013 |title=Borussia Dortmund say Robert Lewandowski will not sign new contract |publisher=Sky Sports |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11899/8529191/Borussia-Dortmund-say-Robert-Lewandowski-will-not-sign-new-contract |access-date=6 March 2013 |archive-date=19 May 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200519022039/https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11899/8529191/Borussia-Dortmund-say-Robert-Lewandowski-will-not-sign-new-contract |url-status=live }}</ref> On 27 February, Lewandowski played in his side's 1–0 defeat to Bayern Munich in the [[2012–13 DFB-Pokal#Quarter-finals|2012–13 DFB-Pokal quarter final]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=27 February 2013 |title=Robben nutzt Schmelzers Tiefschlaf zum Sieg |url=https://www.kicker.de/bayern-gegen-dortmund-2013-dfb-pokal-1877174/analyse |access-date=29 April 2021 |website=[[Kicker (magazine)|Kicker]] |language=de |archive-date=5 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210205213558/https://www.kicker.de/bayern-gegen-dortmund-2013-dfb-pokal-1877174/analyse |url-status=live }}</ref> On 24 April, Lewandowski became the first player to score [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics#Four goals in a match|four goals in a Champions League semi-final]] as Borussia Dortmund defeated Spanish champions [[Real Madrid]] 4–1 in the [[2012–13 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#First leg 3|first leg]] at Dortmund's [[Westfalenstadion|Signal Iduna Park]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=James |first=Andy |date=24 April 2013 |title=Four-goal Lewandowski leaves Madrid reeling |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0208-0e9f60a306c2-d00309689516-1000--four-goal-lewandowski-leaves-madrid-reeling/ |access-date=25 April 2013 |publisher=UEFA |archive-date=24 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190124141005/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/matches/round=2000350/match=2009608/postmatch/report/index.html#four+goal+lewandowski+shines |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Hallam |first=Mark |date=25 April 2013 |title=Dortmund hero Lewandowski linked more loudly with move |publisher=Deutsche Welle |url=http://www.dw.de/dortmund-hero-lewandowski-linked-more-loudly-with-move/a-16772839 |access-date=6 January 2014 |archive-date=16 October 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141016141650/http://www.dw.de/dortmund-hero-lewandowski-linked-more-loudly-with-move/a-16772839 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 25 May, he played in the [[2013 UEFA Champions League Final]] in which Borussia Dortmund were defeated 1–2 by Bayern Munich.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 May 2013 |title=Borussia Dortmund 1–2 Bayern Munich |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/22540468 |access-date=11 May 2014 |publisher=BBC Sport |archive-date=2 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200802214012/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/22540468 |url-status=live }}</ref> He finished the 2012–13 season with 24 league goals, one goal short of the Bundesliga's top scorer, Bayer Leverkusen's [[Stefan Kießling]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/stats/_/league/GER.1/season/2012/german-bundesliga |title=German Bundesliga Scoring Stats - 2012-13 |publisher=ESPN |access-date=11 July 2026 |archive-date=23 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250123201110/https://www.espn.com/soccer/stats/_/league/GER.1/season/2012/german-bundesliga |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Lewandowski 2014.jpg|thumb|upright|left|Lewandowski warming up for [[Borussia Dortmund]] in 2014]]
On 27 July 2013, Lewandowski won the [[2013 DFL-Supercup]] with Dortmund, 4–2, against Bayern Munich.<ref name="2013 German Super Cup">{{Cite web |date=27 July 2013 |title=Dortmund prevail over Bayern in Supercup thriller |url=http://www.bundesliga.com/en/tournaments/news/2013/0000262446.php |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131106163106/http://www.bundesliga.com/en/tournaments/news/2013/0000262446.php |archive-date=6 November 2013 |access-date=5 November 2013 |publisher=bundesliga.com}}</ref> He scored his first goal of the season in Dortmund's 4–0 win over Augsburg in the club's opening Bundesliga match on 10 August.<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 August 2013 |title=Aubameyang hat-trick has Dortmund flying |url=http://www.bundesliga.com/en/liga/news/2013/0000264271.php |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130813011953/http://www.bundesliga.com/en/liga/news/2013/0000264271.php |archive-date=13 August 2013 |access-date=1 May 2014 |publisher=Bundesliga}}</ref> On 1 November, he scored his only hat-trick of the season in a 6–1 Bundesliga win against [[VfB Stuttgart|Stuttgart]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=1 November 2013 |title=Lewandowski shines as Stuttgart hit for six |publisher=bundesliga.com |url=http://www.bundesliga.com/en/liga/news/2013/0000276334.php |url-status=dead |access-date=5 November 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131105001353/http://www.bundesliga.com/en/liga/news/2013/0000276334.php |archive-date=5 November 2013}}</ref>

On 25 February 2014, Lewandowski scored twice in the [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Round of 16|Champions League round of 16]] first leg against [[FC Zenit Saint Petersburg|Zenit Saint Petersburg]], becoming Dortmund's overall top scorer in European competition, surpassing [[Stéphane Chapuisat]]'s 16 goals.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 February 2014 |title=Lewandowski najlepszym strzelcem BVB w Europie! |url=http://sport.wp.pl/kat,1752,title,Lewandowski-najlepszym-strzelcem-BVB-w-Europie,wid,16433212,wiadomosc.html?ticaid=112450 |access-date=25 February 2014 |publisher=sport.wp.pl |language=pl |archive-date=24 October 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141024040320/http://sport.wp.pl/kat,1752,title,Lewandowski-najlepszym-strzelcem-BVB-w-Europie,wid,16433212,wiadomosc.html?ticaid=112450 |url-status=live |last1=Polska |first1=Grupa Wirtualna }}</ref>

He scored his 100th goal for the club on his 182nd appearance, as Dortmund defeated [[VfL Wolfsburg]] 2–0 in the [[2013–14 DFB-Pokal#Semi-finals|semi-finals]] of the [[2013–14 DFB-Pokal]] on 15 April, and revealed a shirt with the number 100 during celebration.<ref>{{Cite news |date=16 April 2014 |title=Lewandowski scores 100th goal for Borussia Dortmund |publisher=[[Polskie Radio]] |url=http://www.thenews.pl/1/5/Artykul/168448,Lewandowski-scores-100th-goal-for-Borussia-Dortmund |access-date=24 October 2014 |archive-date=18 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190418131140/http://www.thenews.pl/1/5/Artykul/168448,Lewandowski-scores-100th-goal-for-Borussia-Dortmund |url-status=live }}</ref>

Lewandowski ended the [[2013–14 Borussia Dortmund season|2013–14 season]] as the top goalscorer in the [[2013–14 Bundesliga|Bundesliga]] with 20 goals, which earned him the {{ill|Torjägerkanone|de}}.<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 May 2014 |title=Lewandowski top of the charts as Dortmund thrash Hertha |url=http://www.bundesliga.com/en/liga/news/2013/0000293681.php |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140511003549/http://www.bundesliga.com/en/liga/news/2013/0000293681.php |archive-date=11 May 2014 |access-date=11 May 2014 |publisher=Bundesliga}}</ref> He also scored six goals in the Champions League, as Dortmund reached the quarter-finals.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Robert Lewandowski |publisher=UEFA |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/clubs/players/250002096--robert-lewandowski/ |access-date=11 May 2014 |archive-date=28 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140428140423/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2014/clubs/player=250002096/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> During the second leg of the round of 16 match between Borussia Dortmund and Zenit, Lewandowski received a second yellow card, which resulted in his suspension for the [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#First leg 2|first leg of the quarter-final]] against Real Madrid.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com.sg/soccer/story/_/id/37341942/borussia-dortmund-manager-jurgen-klopp-calls-robert-lewandowski-yellow-card-laughable |title=Klopp: Lewandowski yellow 'laughable' |publisher=ESPN |date=20 March 2014 }}</ref>

Lewandowski played his final match for Dortmund in the [[2014 DFB-Pokal Final]] against Bayern Munich on 17 May. Head coach [[Jürgen Klopp]] had excused him from some training ahead of the final due to injury concerns; although Lewandowski played all 120 minutes of the final, Dortmund lost, 0–2.<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 May 2014 |title=Bayern Munich and Borussia Dortmund set for German Cup final |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27446201 |access-date=16 May 2014 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |archive-date=18 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190518133335/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27446201 |url-status=live }}</ref> He finished the season with 28 goals in 48 matches.<ref name="2013 German Super Cup" /><ref name=Kicker>{{Cite web |title=Robert Lewandowski |url=https://www.kicker.de/robert-lewandowski/spieler/la-liga/2024-25/fc-barcelona |access-date=6 February 2018 |website=[[Kicker (magazine)|Kicker]] |language=de |archive-date=7 February 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180207062916/http://www.kicker.de/news/fussball/bundesliga/vereine/2013-14/51570/vereinsspieler_robert-lewandowski.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Bayern Munich===
In November 2013, Lewandowski confirmed he would sign a pre-contractual agreement for Borussia Dortmund's rivals Bayern Munich,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Smith |first=Paul |date=10 November 2013 |title=Bayern Munich win the race to sign Borussia Dortmund striker Robert Lewandowski |work=Daily Mirror |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/sport/football/news/robert-lewandowski-bayern-munich-win-2715710 |access-date=5 January 2014 |archive-date=7 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200807182255/https://www.mirror.co.uk/sport/football/news/robert-lewandowski-bayern-munich-win-2715710 |url-status=live }}</ref> which officially happened on 4 January 2014, when he signed a five-year contract beginning at the start of the [[2014–15 FC Bayern Munich season|2014–15 season]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=4 January 2014 |title=Robert Lewandowski to join Bayern Munich from Borussia Dortmund |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/25605262 |access-date=20 July 2017 |publisher=BBC Sport |archive-date=18 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190518140401/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/25605262 |url-status=live }}</ref> Lewandowski was officially presented as a Bayern Munich player on 9 July 2014.<ref name="Unveiled">{{Cite news |last=Badger |first=Jake |date=9 July 2014 |title=In pictures: Bayern Munich introduce Robert Lewandowski |work=The National |url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/sport/in-pictures-bayern-munich-introduce-robert-lewandowski-1.477970 |access-date=7 June 2020 |archive-date=7 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200607163152/https://www.thenational.ae/sport/in-pictures-bayern-munich-introduce-robert-lewandowski-1.477970 |url-status=live }}</ref>

====2014–2015: Third Bundesliga title====
[[File:CSKA-Bavaria (4).jpg|thumb|upright|right|Lewandowski playing for [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in 2014]]

Pre-season started on 9 July 2014<ref>{{Cite news |date=20 May 2014 |title=Muskelfaserriss: Saison-Aus für Alaba |language=de |work=[[Österreich (newspaper)|Österreich]] |url=http://sport.oe24.at/fussball/Muskelfaserriss-Saison-Aus-fuer-Alaba/144058076 |access-date=20 May 2014 |archive-date=1 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190401150443/https://sport.oe24.at/fussball/Muskelfaserriss-Saison-Aus-fuer-Alaba/144058076 |url-status=live }}</ref> at which time he was presented.<ref name="Unveiled"/> He made his pre–season debut against [[MSV Duisburg]] on 21 July, scoring a goal in the process.<ref>{{Cite news |date=21 July 2014 |title=Ein Treffer zum Einstand |language=de |work=[[Süddeutsche Zeitung]] |url=http://www.sueddeutsche.de/sport/lewandowski-debuet-beim-fc-bayern-ein-treffer-zum-einstand-1.2056832 |access-date=24 July 2014 |archive-date=17 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200217161652/https://www.sueddeutsche.de/sport/lewandowski-debuet-beim-fc-bayern-ein-treffer-zum-einstand-1.2056832 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 6 August, he opened the scoring as Bayern contested the [[2014 MLS All-Star Game]] at the [[Providence Park]] in [[Portland, Oregon]], eventually losing 1–2.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Itel |first=Dan |date=7 August 2014 |title=MLS 2, Bayern Munich 1 &#124; AT&T MLS All-Star Game Recap |url=https://www.mlssoccer.com/history/mls-all-star-game/2014 |access-date=7 June 2020 |publisher=[[Major League Soccer]] |archive-date=1 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200601061424/https://www.mlssoccer.com/history/mls-all-star-game/2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

He made his competitive debut for his new club in a 0–2 loss to Borussia Dortmund in the [[2014 DFL-Supercup]] on 13 August 2014,<ref name="2014 German Super Cup">{{Cite web |date=13 August 2014 |title=Aubameyang köpft BVB zum Supercup-Sieg |trans-title=Aubameyang heads BVB to the Supercup victory |url=https://www.kicker.de/dortmund-gegen-bayern-2014-dfl-supercup-2396815/analyse |access-date=30 August 2014 |website=[[Kicker (magazine)|Kicker]] |language=de |archive-date=1 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190401144901/http://www.kicker.de/d-supercup-fb-1/2014/1/2396815/spielbericht_borussia-dortmund-17_bayern-muenchen-14.html |url-status=live }}</ref> and scored his first goal in a 1–1 draw against Schalke 04 in his second league match on 30 August.<ref>{{Cite web |date=30 August 2014 |title=Bayern Munich denied win as Schalke claim 1–1 draw in Alonso debut |url=http://www.espnfc.us/german-bundesliga/story/2012895/bayern-munich-denied-win-as-schalke-claim-1-1-draw-in-alonso-debut |access-date=30 August 2014 |publisher=ESPN FC |agency=PA Sports |archive-date=17 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200217161652/http://www.espnfc.us/german-bundesliga/story/2012895/bayern-munich-denied-win-as-schalke-claim-1-1-draw-in-alonso-debut |url-status=dead }}</ref> On 21 October, Lewandowski scored his first Champions League goal for Bayern Munich in a [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League group stage#Roma v Bayern Munich|7–1 away win]] against [[A.S. Roma|Roma]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 October 2020 |title=Roma 1–7 Bayern Munich |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/29362752 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=6 October 2020 |archive-date=12 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112040449/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/29362752 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 1 November, in his first league match against Dortmund, Lewandowski scored in a 2–1 win which put Bayern four points clear at the top of the table while leaving his former club in a relegation play-off place.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 November 2014 |title=Bayern Munich 2–1 Borussia Dortmund |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/29848613 |access-date=1 November 2014 |publisher=BBC Sport |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121212248/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/29848613 |url-status=live }}</ref> In his third match of the season against Dortmund on 4 April 2015, Lewandowski scored in the 36th minute in a 1–0 win, after Dortmund's goalkeeper [[Roman Weidenfeller]] "parried" [[Thomas Müller]]'s shot.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rosner |first=Maik |date=4 April 2015 |title=Lewandowski setzt die Pointe |language=de |trans-title=Lewandowski sets the punch line |work=Süddeutsche Zeitung |location=Dortmund |url=https://www.sueddeutsche.de/sport/bayern-sieg-gegen-dortmund-lewandowski-setzt-die-pointe-1.2422765 |access-date=4 April 2015 |archive-date=19 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191219125301/https://www.sueddeutsche.de/sport/bayern-sieg-gegen-dortmund-lewandowski-setzt-die-pointe-1.2422765 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=4 April 2015 |title=Borussia Dortmund 0–1 Bayern Munich |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/32121014 |access-date=4 April 2015 |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121212248/http://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/32121014 |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 21 February 2015, Lewandowski scored twice in Bayern's 6–0 win away at [[SC Paderborn 07|Paderborn]], his second goal of the game was his 10th of the league season.<ref>{{Cite news |date=21 February 2015 |title=Lewan helps Bayern crush Paderborn |publisher=[[SuperSport (South African broadcaster)|Supersport]] |url=http://www.supersport.com/football/germany/news/150221/Lewan_helps_Bayern_crush_Paderborn |access-date=7 March 2015 |archive-date=13 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180913223343/https://www.supersport.com/football/germany/news/150221/Lewan_helps_Bayern_crush_Paderborn |url-status=live }}</ref> He scored twice in the first half on 21 April as Bayern [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Bayern Munich v Porto|overturned]] a [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Quarter-finals|deficit from the first leg]] to defeat [[FC Porto|Porto]] 7–4 on aggregate and advance to the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Semi-finals|semi-finals of the Champions League]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Uersfeld |first=Stephan |date=21 April 2015 |title=Bayern Munich 6–1 FC Porto |publisher=ESPN |url=http://www.espnfc.us/uefa-champions-league/2/blog/post/2410074/bayern-munich-uses-a-breathtaking-first-half-to-crush-portowrites-stephan-uersfeld |access-date=21 April 2015 |archive-date=13 August 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180813004736/http://www.espnfc.us/uefa-champions-league/2/blog/post/2410074/bayern-munich-uses-a-breathtaking-first-half-to-crush-portowrites-stephan-uersfeld |url-status=live }}</ref> Five days later, after VfL Wolfsburg lost to Borussia Mönchengladbach, Bayern won the Bundesliga title.<ref>{{cite news |author=<!--Staff writer(s)/no by-line.--> |title=Bayern Munich: Pep Guardiola's side win club's 25th German title |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/32475479 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |location=[[City of Salford|Salford]] |date=26 April 2015 |access-date=26 April 2015 |archive-date=27 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160327110618/http://www.bbc.com/sport/football/32475479 |url-status=live }}</ref> He scored again on 28 April, opening a 1–1 draw in the [[2014–15 DFB-Pokal#Bayern Munich v Borussia Dortmund|DFB-Pokal semi-final]] against Dortmund, but was later involved in a mid-air collision with [[Mitchell Langerak]] in the 116th minute of [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]].<ref>{{cite news |author=<!--Staff writer(s)/no by-line.--> |title=Arjen Robben hurt in Bayern's loss, Robert Lewandowski concussed |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/37414495/arjen-robben-hurt-bayern-loss-robert-lewandowski-concu |work=[[ESPN]] |location=[[Bristol, Connecticut]] |publisher=[[ESPN Inc.|ESPN Enterprises]] |date=29 April 2015 |access-date=20 August 2021 |archive-date=30 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231030215207/https://ssum-sec.casalemedia.com/usermatch?us_privacy=1YNY&d=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.espn.com%2F&s=184674&cb=https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.indexww.com%2Fht%2Fhtw-pixel.gif%3F&C=1 |url-status=live }}</ref> The game ended in Bayern's elimination via a penalty shoot-out (0–2), and, unusually, none of the four attempts were converted by the Munich side, at their own stadium.<ref>{{cite news |author=<!--Staff writer(s)/no by-line.--> |title=German Cup: Borussia Dortmund beat Bayern Munich on penalties |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/32484226 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |location=[[City of Salford|Salford]] |date=28 April 2015 |access-date=29 April 2015 |archive-date=25 August 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160825185804/http://www.bbc.com/sport/football/32484226 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Harding |first=Jonathan |date=28 April 2015 |title=Dortmund beat Bayern in shoot-out drama to make German Cup final |url=https://www.dw.com/en/dortmund-beat-bayern-in-shoot-out-drama-to-make-german-cup-final/a-18416115 |work=[[Deutsche Welle]] |location=[[Bonn]] |access-date=20 August 2021 |archive-date=20 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210820113736/https://www.dw.com/en/dortmund-beat-bayern-in-shoot-out-drama-to-make-german-cup-final/a-18416115 |url-status=live }}</ref> Although Lewandowski stayed until the end of the match, he didn't participate in the shootout; and tests later confirmed that he had fractured [[Mandibular fracture|jaw]] and [[Nasal fracture|nose]] bone, and had a [[concussion]], ruling him out for approximately one week.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2015/04/robben-out-for-season-lewy-facial-injury |title=Season over for Robben, Lewandowski badly hurt |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=29 April 2015 |publisher=[[FC Bayern Munich]] |access-date=20 August 2021 |archive-date=20 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210820113736/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2015/04/robben-out-for-season-lewy-facial-injury |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |author=<!--Staff writer(s)/no by-line.--> |title=Rzecznik Bayernu: Lewandowski ma złamaną kość szczęki, nosa i wstrząśnienie mózgu |trans-title=Bayern spokesman: Lewandowski has a fractured jaw bone, nose's and concussion |url=https://www.polsatsport.pl/wiadomosc/2015-04-29/rzecznik-bayernu-lewandowski-ma-zlamana-kosc-szczeki-nosa-i-wstrzasnienie-mozgu/ |language=pl |work=[[Polsat Sport]] |location=Warsaw |publisher=[[Cyfrowy Polsat]] |date=29 April 2015 |access-date=20 August 2021 |archive-date=20 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210820113739/https://www.polsatsport.pl/wiadomosc/2015-04-29/rzecznik-bayernu-lewandowski-ma-zlamana-kosc-szczeki-nosa-i-wstrzasnienie-mozgu/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2015/05/news-in-brief-040515 |title=Masked Lewandowski able to train |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=4 May 2015 |publisher=[[FC Bayern Munich]] |access-date=20 August 2021 |archive-date=20 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210820115238/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2015/05/news-in-brief-040515 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 12 May, playing in a protective mask, he [[Curl (association football)|curled in]] at the 59th minute in his team's 3–2 home victory against eventual winners [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] in the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Bayern Munich v Barcelona|Champions League semifinal second leg]], albeit they were eliminated by an aggregate score of 3–5.<ref>{{cite news |last=Jurejko |first=Jonathan |date=12 May 2015 |title=Bayern Munich 3–2 Barcelona (agg 3–5) |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/32683310 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |location=[[City of Salford|Salford]] |access-date=24 April 2021 |archive-date=8 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190408101217/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/32683310 |url-status=live }}</ref> With 17 goals in 31 games, Lewandowski was joint-second highest scorer of the Bundesliga season alongside teammate [[Arjen Robben]], behind [[Eintracht Frankfurt]]'s [[Alexander Meier]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=23 May 2015 |title=Injured Meier is Germany's top-scorer |work=[[The Times of India]] |agency=Agence France-Presse |url=http://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/sports/football/top-stories/Injured-Meier-is-Germanys-top-scorer/articleshow/47402785.cms |access-date=26 May 2015 |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121212248/http://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/sports/football/top-stories/Injured-Meier-is-Germanys-top-scorer/articleshow/47402785.cms |url-status=live }}</ref> He finished the season with 25 goals in 49 appearances.<ref name="2014 German Super Cup" /><ref name=Kicker/>

====2015–2017: Domestic success, Torjägerkanone, and 100 Bayern goals====
[[File:Robert Lewandowski Training FC Bayern München-2.jpg|thumb|upright|right|Lewandowski training with [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in 2015]]

Lewandowski's [[2015–16 FC Bayern Munich season|second season]] began with the [[2015 DFL-Supercup]] on 1 August, with Bayern losing in a penalty shootout away to VfL Wolfsburg; he had been substituted in the 72nd minute for [[Rafinha (footballer, born 1985)|Rafinha]].<ref name="supercup">{{Cite news |last=Dunbar |first=Ross |date=1 August 2015 |title=Bendtner leads Wolfsburg over Bayern Munich on penalties in German Super Cup clash |work=Fox Sports |url=http://www.foxsports.com/soccer/story/bendtner-leads-wolfsburg-over-bayern-munich-on-penalties-in-german-super-cup-clash-080115?vid=null |access-date=3 August 2015 |archive-date=3 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180703050953/https://www.foxsports.com/soccer/story/bendtner-leads-wolfsburg-over-bayern-munich-on-penalties-in-german-super-cup-clash-080115?vid=null |url-status=live }}</ref> Eight days later in the [[2015–16 DFB-Pokal#FC Nöttingen v Bayern Munich|DFB-Pokal first round match]], he scored the last goal in a 3–1 win against [[Oberliga Baden-Württemberg]] club [[FC Nöttingen|Nöttingen]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=9 August 2015 |title=Nottingen 1–3 Bayern Munich: Vidal off the mark in cup win |url=http://www.goal.com/en/match/n%C3%B6ttingen-vs-bayern-m%C3%BCnchen/2038931/report?ICID=MP_MS_5 |access-date=14 August 2015 |website=Goal |publisher=Perform Group |location=Leeds |archive-date=24 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924161936/http://www.goal.com/en/match/n%C3%B6ttingen-vs-bayern-m%C3%BCnchen/2038931/report?ICID=MP_MS_5 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 14 August, in the opening match of the [[2015–16 Bundesliga|new Bundesliga season]], he scored the second goal of a 5–0 win over Hamburg.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 August 2015 |title=Bayern Munich 5–0 Hamburg: Bavarians begin Bundesliga campaign in ominous fashion |url=http://www.goal.com/en-gb/match/bayern-m%C3%BCnchen-vs-hamburger-sv/2054191?ICID=HP_HN_HP_RI_1_1 |access-date=14 August 2015 |website=Goal |publisher=Perform Group |location=Leeds |archive-date=24 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924115509/http://www.goal.com/en-gb/match/bayern-m%C3%BCnchen-vs-hamburger-sv/2054191?ICID=HP_HN_HP_RI_1_1 |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 22 September 2015, Lewandowski set a [[Bundesliga records and statistics#Goals 2|Bundesliga record]] by coming on as a substitute with Bayern trailing 0–1 to Wolfsburg and scoring five goals in 8 minutes and 59 seconds, the fastest by any player in Bundesliga history, to take a 5–1 lead. He also set Bundesliga records for the fastest hat-trick (three goals in four minutes), and most goals scored by a substitute (five).<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 September 2015 |title=Bayern striker Robert Lewandowski scores 5 goals in 9 minutes |url=http://www.espnfc.com/bayern-munich/story/2625497/bayern-robert-lewandowski-scores-five-in-nine-minutes |access-date=22 September 2015 |publisher=[[ESPN FC]] |archive-date=14 February 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190214100456/http://www.espnfc.com/bayern-munich/story/2625497/bayern-robert-lewandowski-scores-five-in-nine-minutes |url-status=live }}</ref> Lewandowski's five goals in nine minutes was also the fastest in any [[Big Five (association football)|major European football league]] since [[Opta Sports|Opta]] began keeping records, and it ended Wolfsburg's 14-match unbeaten run.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 September 2015 |title=Robert Lewandowski: More goals in nine minutes than Liverpool |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/34332408 |access-date=22 September 2015 |publisher=BBC Sport |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121212248/http://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/34332408 |url-status=live }}</ref> He was awarded four certificates by [[Guinness World Records]] for this feat.<ref name=guinness>{{Cite web |date=1 December 2015 |title=Robert Lewandowski receives awards for five-goal feat |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34979052 |access-date=1 December 2015 |publisher=BBC Sport |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121212248/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/34979052 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Four days later, he scored twice in a 3–0 win at [[1. FSV Mainz 05|Mainz]], the first goal being his 100th Bundesliga goal on his 168th appearance, a league record for a foreign player. He also reached 10 goals in the opening 7 matches with this brace, a feat only achieved before by [[Gerd Müller]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=26 September 2015 |title=1. FSV Mainz 05 0–3 Bayern Munich |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34351078 |access-date=26 September 2015 |publisher=BBC Sport |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121212248/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/34351078 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 29 September, he scored a [[List of UEFA Champions League hat-tricks|Champions League hat-trick]] in a 5–0 win over [[Dinamo Zagreb]], putting him on ten goals in three games in a week.<ref>{{Cite news |date=29 September 2015 |title=Bayern Munich 5–0 Dinamo Zagreb |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34275527 |access-date=30 September 2015 |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121212248/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/34275527 |url-status=live }}</ref> He added two in a 5–1 rout of Dortmund five days later, to total 12 goals in his last four appearances.<ref>{{Cite news |date=4 October 2015 |title=Bayern Munich 5–1 Borussia Dortmund |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34381544 |access-date=5 October 2015 |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121212248/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/34381544 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 24 October, Lewandowski scored in a 4–0 home win over Köln, a result which made Bayern the first Bundesliga team ever to win all 10 of their opening games of a season.<ref>{{Cite news |date=24 October 2015 |title=Bayern Munich 4–0 1. FC Köln |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34619451 |access-date=24 October 2015 |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121212248/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/34619451 |url-status=live }}</ref> The victory in Cologne was also Bayern's 1,000th win in the Bundesliga.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 October 2015 |title=Erfolg gegen Köln: Bayern feiert 1000. Sieg in der Bundesliga |trans-title=Bayern celebrates 1000th victory in the Bundesliga |url=https://www.spiegel.de/sport/fussball/bayern-muenchen-feiert-1000-bundesliga-sieg-a-1059451.html |access-date=31 October 2015 |website=Der Spiegel |language=de |archive-date=15 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160315084150/http://www.spiegel.de/sport/fussball/bayern-muenchen-feiert-1000-bundesliga-sieg-a-1059451.html |url-status=live }}</ref> On 11 January 2016, he achieved fourth place at the [[2015 FIFA Ballon d'Or]] awards.<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 October 2015 |title=Men's Football shortlists for FIFA Ballon d'Or 2015 revealed |url=https://www.fifa.com/ballon-dor/news/y=2015/m=10/news=men-s-football-shortlists-for-fifa-ballon-d-or-2015-revealed-2719188.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151021220908/http://www.fifa.com/ballon-dor/news/y=2015/m=10/news=men-s-football-shortlists-for-fifa-ballon-d-or-2015-revealed-2719188.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=21 October 2015 |publisher=[[FIFA]]}}</ref>

On 19 March 2016, Lewandowski scored the only goal in a 1–0 win against Köln to bring his league total up to 25 goals; a new personal best.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 March 2016 |title=1. FC Koln 0–1 Bayern Munich |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/35803485 |access-date=19 March 2016 |publisher=BBC Sport |archive-date=18 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190418032306/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/35803485 |url-status=live }}</ref> He had scored 24 goals for Borussia Dortmund during the 2012–13 season.<ref>{{Cite news |date=19 March 2016 |title=Robert Lewandowski takes Bayern Munich eight clear with win over Köln |work=The Guardian |agency=Associated Press |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/mar/19/koln-bayern-munich-bundesliga-match-report |access-date=20 March 2016 |archive-date=20 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160320072055/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/mar/19/koln-bayern-munich-bundesliga-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> He also started Bayern's [[Comeback (sports)#Association football|comeback]] with a 73rd-minute header in the [[2015–16 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Round of 16|second leg of the round of 16]] on 16 March, after trailing 0–2 home to [[Juventus FC|Juventus]], which Munich eventually won 4–2 after extra time, and 6–4 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news |last=Lewis |first=Aimee |date=16 March 2016 |title=Bayern Munich 4-2 Juventus (agg 6-4) |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/35814918 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |location=[[City of Salford|Salford]] |access-date=20 August 2021 |archive-date=10 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210810092237/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/35814918 |url-status=live }}</ref> His goal against [[Atlético Madrid]] on 3 May in the second leg of Bayern's [[2015–16 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Semi-finals|Champions League semifinal]] exit saw him end the season's competition with nine goals.<ref>{{Cite news |date=3 May 2016 |title=Griezmann's away goal sends Atletico into final |work=Capital FM |url=http://www.capitalfm.co.ke/sports/2016/05/03/griezmanns-away-goal-sends-atletico-into-final/ |access-date=14 May 2016 |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806225142/http://www.capitalfm.co.ke/sports/2016/05/03/griezmanns-away-goal-sends-atletico-into-final/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 7 May 2016, Lewandowski scored both goals for Bayern in a 2–1 win at [[FC Ingolstadt 04|Ingolstadt]] to confirm the Bavarian club as [[List of German football champions|champions of Germany]] for the fourth consecutive season.<ref>{{Cite web |date=7 May 2016 |title=FC Ingolstadt 04 1–2 Bayern Munich |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36187452 |access-date=14 May 2016 |publisher=BBC Sport |archive-date=7 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180707040221/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36187452 |url-status=live }}</ref> A week later, he scored his 30th goal of the season in Bayern's final league match of the season at home to [[Hannover 96]]. This made him the first foreign player to score 30 goals in the Bundesliga, the first player since [[Dieter Müller]] in [[1976–77 Bundesliga|1976–77]], and secured him the Torjägerkanone for the second time in three seasons.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 May 2016 |title=Bayern Munich 3–1 Hannover 96 |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36250248 |access-date=14 May 2016 |publisher=BBC Sport |archive-date=7 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180707043253/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36250248 |url-status=live }}</ref> He finished the season with 42 goals in 51 matches.<ref name=supercup/><ref name=Kicker/>

The [[2016–17 FC Bayern Munich season|2016–17 season]] started with Bayern winning the [[2016 DFL-Supercup]] on 14 August.<ref name="2016 German Super Cup">{{Cite web |title=Im zweiten Anlauf: Vidal beschert Bayern den ersten Titel |url=https://www.kicker.de/dortmund-gegen-bayern-2016-dfl-supercup-3303617/analyse |access-date=20 August 2016 |website=[[Kicker (magazine)|Kicker]] |language=de |archive-date=2 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180702095338/http://www.kicker.de/news/fussball/bundesliga/startseite/d-supercup-fb-1/2016/1/3303617/spielbericht_borussia-dortmund-17_bayern-muenchen-14.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Five days later, Bayern defeated [[FC Carl Zeiss Jena|Carl Zeiss Jena]] 5–0 in the [[2016–17 DFB-Pokal#First round|DFB-Pokal first round]], with the help of Lewandowski's hat-trick during the first half and assist to [[Arturo Vidal]] in the 72nd minute.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 August 2016 |title=FC Carl Zeiss Jena 0–5 Bayern Munich |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36947756 |access-date=20 August 2016 |publisher=BBC Sport |archive-date=13 August 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180813212021/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36947756 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=19 August 2016 |title=FC Carl Zeiss Jena vs. Bayern Munich – Football Match full Commentary |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/match?gameId=452455 |access-date=25 August 2016 |publisher=[[ESPN FC]] |archive-date=14 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191214061250/https://www.espn.com/soccer/match?gameId=452455 |url-status=live }}</ref> He opened the [[2016–17 Bundesliga]] season with another hat-trick in a 6–0 victory against Werder Bremen.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lovell |first=Mark |date=26 August 2016 |title=Bayern cruise to season-opening win on back of Lewandowski hat trick |url=http://www.espnfc.com/club/bayern-munich/132/blog/post/2938084/bayern-cruise-to-season-opening-win-on-back-of-lewandowski-hat-trick |access-date=27 August 2016 |publisher=ESPN |archive-date=13 August 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180813210835/http://www.espnfc.com/club/bayern-munich/132/blog/post/2938084/bayern-cruise-to-season-opening-win-on-back-of-lewandowski-hat-trick |url-status=live }}</ref> On 13 December, Lewandowski signed a new contract with Bayern, keeping him at the club until 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 December 2016 |title=Lewandowski extends stay at Bayern |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2016/12/press-release-robert-lewandowski-extends-contract |access-date=13 December 2016 |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806215805/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2016/12/press-release-robert-lewandowski-extends-contract |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 11 March 2017, Lewandowski reached 100 goals for Bayern in his 137th appearance for the club, scoring twice in a 3–0 victory against Eintracht Frankfurt in the Bundesliga.<ref>{{Cite news |date=11 March 2017 |title=Lewandowski joins Bayern Munich's 100 club |work=FourFourTwo |url=http://www.fourfourtwo.com/news/lewandowski-joins-bayern-munichs-100-club |access-date=13 March 2017 |archive-date=4 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180704124905/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/news/lewandowski-joins-bayern-munichs-100-club |url-status=live }}</ref> He finished the season with 42 goals in 47 matches.<ref name="2016 German Super Cup" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Robert Lewandowski |url=https://www.kicker.de/robert-lewandowski/spieler/bundesliga/2016-17/fc-bayern-muenchen |access-date=6 February 2018 |website=[[Kicker (magazine)|Kicker]] |language=de |archive-date=18 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190418025217/https://www.kicker.de/news/fussball/bundesliga/vereine/1-bundesliga/2016-17/bayern-muenchen-14/51570/spieler_robert-lewandowski.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

====2017–2019: Consecutive Golden Boots and all-time foreign Bundesliga goalscorer====
[[File:Robert Lewandowski Training 2017-03 FC Bayern Muenchen-3.jpg|thumb|upright|right|Lewandowski training with [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in 2017]]

The [[2017–18 FC Bayern Munich season|season]] began with Bayern Munich winning the [[2017 DFL-Supercup]] against Borussia Dortmund, in which Lewandowski scored the opening goal for the Bavarians by controlling a low cross from [[Joshua Kimmich]] to cancel out [[Christian Pulisic]]'s opener. The match ended 2–2 after extra time. Lewandowski, again, scored the first penalty of the shootout as Bayern eventually won 5–4.<ref>{{Cite web |date=6 August 2017 |title=Bayern Munich beat Dortmund on penalties to retain Supercup |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/borussia-dortmund-bayern-munich-supercup-line-ups-and-statistics-456360.jsp |access-date=7 June 2020 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806190635/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/borussia-dortmund-bayern-munich-supercup-line-ups-and-statistics-456360.jsp |url-status=live }}</ref>

Lewandowski started from where he left in the last season and once again was the top scorer in the early stages of the [[2017–18 Bundesliga]]. On 13 December, in the league fixture against Köln, he scored the only goal of the game, to reach Bundesliga's top ten goalscorers of all time.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 December 2017 |title=Robert Lewandowski moves into the top 10 on the Bundesliga's all-time goalscoring list |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/robert-lewandowski-into-top-10-on-the-bundesliga-s-all-time-goalscoring-list-464867.jsp |access-date=15 December 2017 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806172802/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/robert-lewandowski-into-top-10-on-the-bundesliga-s-all-time-goalscoring-list-464867.jsp |url-status=live }}</ref> A couple of months later, on Matchday 22, Lewandowski again found the back of the net against Schalke 04 at the [[Allianz Arena]] to equal the record of scoring in 11 successive home games in a single season, a record also held by then Bayern manager [[Jupp Heynckes]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=10 February 2018 |title=Robert Lewandowski equals record of scoring in 11 successive home games |work=Marca |location=Spain |url=https://www.marca.com/en/football/international-football/2018/02/10/5a7f3d72e2704eee108b459f.html |access-date=11 February 2018 |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806163908/https://amp.marca.com/en/football/international-football/2018/02/10/5a7f3d72e2704eee108b459f.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He continued his goal scoring form by netting a hat-trick against Hamburg as the runaway leaders won 6–0, while he also missed a kick from the spot which would have been his fourth goal of the day. This was his first penalty missed for Bayern in the Bundesliga, nevertheless he scored the second spot kick to complete his hat-trick.<ref>{{Cite news |date=10 March 2018 |title=Bundesliga round-up: Robert Lewandowski's hat-trick edges Bayern Munich closer to the title |work=Sky sports |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11881/11285045/bundesliga-round-up-robert-lewandowskis-hat-trick-edges-bayern-munich-closer-to-the-title |access-date=11 March 2018 |archive-date=11 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200811092809/https://www.skysports.com/amp/football/news/11881/11285045/bundesliga-round-up-robert-lewandowski%27s-hat-trick-edges-bayern-munich-closer-to-the-title/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 11 February 2018, he was voted Poland's Footballer of the Year for the seventh time in a row.<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 February 2018 |title=Lewandowski Footballer of the Year again |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2018/02/lewandowski-named-polish-footballer-of-the-year-again |access-date=4 March 2018 |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806215809/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2018/02/lewandowski-named-polish-footballer-of-the-year-again |url-status=live }}</ref> On 22 February, he fired his long-time agent, Cezary Kucharski. Lewandowski hired renowned dealmaker [[Pini Zahavi]] as his new agent; the hiring of Zahavi was rumoured to be the start of Lewandowski trying to seal a summer move to Real Madrid.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Uersfeld |first=Stephen |date=22 February 2018 |title=Robert Lewandowski splits from agent amid Real Madrid links – sources |url=https://global.espn.com/football/story/_/id/37546327/robert-lewandowski-splits-agent-amid-real-madrid-links-sources |access-date=4 March 2018 |publisher=[[ESPN]] |archive-date=6 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181206175944/http://global.espn.com/football/bayern-munich/story/3393508/robert-lewandowski-splits-from-agent-amid-real-madrid-links-sources |url-status=live }}</ref> On 24 February, he played his 250th Bundesliga game against [[Hertha BSC|Hertha Berlin]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 February 2018 |title=Coman sustains capsular injury |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2018/02/news-in-brief-240218 |access-date=4 March 2018 |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806215825/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2018/02/news-in-brief-240218 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 19 May, Lewandowski scored Bayern's only goal in a 3–1 defeat in the [[2018 DFB-Pokal Final|DFB-Pokal Final]] against Eintracht Frankfurt.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Match Report |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/matches/profis/dfb-pokal/2017-2018/fc-bayern-muenchen-eintracht-frankfurt-19-05-2018-matchreport |access-date=12 August 2018 |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |archive-date=7 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200807033739/https://fcbayern.com/en/matches/profis/dfb-pokal/2017-2018/fc-bayern-muenchen-eintracht-frankfurt-19-05-2018-matchreport |url-status=live }}</ref>

Lewandowski finished the league as the Bundesliga's top goalscorer with 29 goals. This was the third time he won the Torjägerkanone award.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 May 2018 |title=Robert Lewandowski, Thomas Müller and the Bundesliga's 2017/18 chart-toppers |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/lewandowski-thomas-muller-2017-18-most-goals-most-assists-top-stats-481046.jsp |access-date=20 May 2018 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806204630/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/lewandowski-thomas-muller-2017-18-most-goals-most-assists-top-stats-481046.jsp |url-status=live }}</ref> He finished the season with 41 goals in 48 matches in all competitions.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The 10 top goalscorers of 2017/18 in all competitions |url=https://www.besoccer.com/new/the-10-top-goalscorers-of-2017-18-in-all-competitions-436024 |access-date=30 May 2018 |website=BeSoccer |archive-date=7 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200807030507/https://www.besoccer.com/new/the-10-top-goalscorers-of-2017-18-in-all-competitions-436024 |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 1 August, after a summer of transfer speculation, Bayern CEO [[Karl-Heinz Rummenigge]], confirmed in an interview that Lewandowski would not be allowed to leave Bayern at any price, saying "the top quality we have at Bayern Munich will stay here. With Robert, we clearly want to send a signal to people within and outside the club: Bayern Munich are completely different to other clubs who get weak when certain sums are mentioned"<ref>{{Cite web |title=Robert Lewandowski is not for sale at any price, says Bayern Munich chief executive |url=http://www.skysports.com/football/news/11890/11456519/robert-lewandowski-is-not-for-sale-at-any-price-says-bayern-munich-chief-executive |access-date=12 August 2018 |website=Sky Sports |archive-date=5 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180905211426/http://www.skysports.com/football/news/11890/11456519/robert-lewandowski-is-not-for-sale-at-any-price-says-bayern-munich-chief-executive |url-status=live }}</ref> On 12 August, Lewandowski recorded the first ever hat-trick in the [[DFL-Supercup]] in a 0–5 away victory against Eintracht Frankfurt in the [[2018 DFL-Supercup|2018 edition]] as Bayern Munich went on to win the title for the record seventh time.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Robert Lewandowski hat trick leads Bayern Munich to Super Cup win |url=https://www.dw.com/en/robert-lewandowski-hat-trick-leads-bayern-munich-to-super-cup-win/a-45054001 |access-date=12 August 2018 |publisher=Deutsche Welle |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806184652/https://www.dw.com/en/robert-lewandowski-hat-trick-leads-bayern-munich-to-super-cup-win/a-45054001 |url-status=live }}</ref> He also became the [[DFL-Supercup#Top goalscorers|all-time top scorer]] in the German Supercup history.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Eintracht Frankfurt 0 Bayern Munich 5: Lewandowski hat-trick gets Kovac off to a flyer |url=http://www.goal.com/en-ng/news/4055/mamain/2018/08/12/46973872/eintracht-frankfurt-0-bayern-munich-5-lewandowski-hat-trick-gets- |access-date=13 August 2018 |website=Goal |publisher=DAZN Group |location=Leeds |archive-date=15 August 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180815114745/http://www.goal.com/en-ng/news/4055/mamain/2018/08/12/46973872/eintracht-frankfurt-0-bayern-munich-5-lewandowski-hat-trick-gets- |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=All-time top goalscorers |url=http://www.worldfootball.net/alltime_goalgetter/supercup/tore/1/ |website=WorldFootball.net |access-date=13 August 2018 |archive-date=13 August 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180813175440/http://www.worldfootball.net/alltime_goalgetter/supercup/tore/1/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

On 27 November, Lewandowski became the third-fastest player to score 50 goals (after [[Lionel Messi]] and [[Ruud van Nistelrooy]]) in the Champions League, when he scored two goals in a 5–1 [[2018–19 UEFA Champions League group stage#Group E|group stage]] home win over [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]]. It took Lewandowski just 77 Champions League matches to reach the milestone.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Robert Lewandowski becomes third-fastest player after Lionel Messi and Ruud van Nistelrooy to reach 50 UEFA Champions League goals |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/lewandowski-3rd-fastest-to-50-champions-league-goals-after-messi-va-nistelrooy-517997.jsp |access-date=29 November 2018 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806204424/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/lewandowski-3rd-fastest-to-50-champions-league-goals-after-messi-va-nistelrooy-517997.jsp |url-status=live }}</ref> He finished as the top scorer in the Champions League group stage with eight goals in six matches.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lewandowski top scorer in Champions League group stage |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/024c-0e994abca367-5918ce3eca39-1000--lewandowski-top-scorer-in-champions-league-group-stage/ |access-date=13 December 2018 |publisher=UEFA |archive-date=18 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181218070543/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2586334.html?iv=true |url-status=live }}</ref> On 9 February 2019, Lewandowski scored in a 3–1 win over Schalke 04 and became the first player to score 100 competitive goals at the Allianz Arena. His goal was also his 119th league goal for Bayern Munich, which saw him draw level with [[Roland Wohlfarth]] as the club's third-highest goalscorer of all-time.<ref>{{Cite web |date=9 February 2019 |title=Robert Lewandowski scores milestone goal as Bayern Munich climb back up to second with Schalke win |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/bayern-munich-schalke-live-line-ups-stats-james-rodriguez-neuer-lewandowski-3168-2047 |access-date=10 February 2019 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806194017/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-schalke-live-line-ups-stats-james-rodriguez-neuer-lewandowski-3168-2047 |url-status=live }}</ref>

He surpassed Wohlfarth the following month after scoring a brace in a 5–1 win over Borussia Mönchengladbach, with his second goal also seeing him equal [[Claudio Pizarro]]'s record of 195 league goals for the most Bundesliga goals by a foreign player.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2 March 2019 |title=Bayern Munich go level with Borussia Dortmund after crushing win over Borussia Mönchengladbach |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/borussia-monchengladbach-bayern-munich-live-line-ups-stats-james-rodriguez-coman-hazard-2607 |access-date=2 March 2019 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=7 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200807035424/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/borussia-monchengladbach-bayern-munich-live-line-ups-stats-james-rodriguez-coman-hazard-2607 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2 March 2019 |title=Robert Lewandowski, Claudio Pizarro and the Bundesliga's best-ever foreign scorers |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/best-foreign-scorer-robert-lewandowski-claudio-pizarro-bayern-munich-2710-1129 |access-date=2 March 2019 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806200407/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/best-foreign-scorer-robert-lewandowski-claudio-pizarro-bayern-munich-2710-1129 |url-status=live }}</ref> In his very next fixture, he broke Pizarro's record by scoring twice in a 6–0 win over Wolfsburg.<ref>{{Cite web |date=9 March 2019 |title=Bayern Munich thump Wolfsburg to go top on historic afternoon for Robert Lewandowski |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/bayern-munich-wolfsburg-live-line-ups-stats-james-rodriguez-lewandowski-brooks-2804 |access-date=9 March 2019 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=7 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200807043016/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-wolfsburg-live-line-ups-stats-james-rodriguez-lewandowski-brooks-2804 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 6 April, in the 100th Bundesliga meeting between Bayern Munich and Dortmund, Lewandowski scored twice in a 5–0 win, with his first goal taking him to 200 goals in the league.<ref>{{Cite news |date=6 April 2019 |title=Bayern Munich thrash Borussia Dortmund to regain control of Bundesliga title race |publisher=Bundesliga |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/bayern-munich-borussia-dortmund-live-line-ups-stats-james-rodriguez-lewandowski-3439 |access-date=7 April 2019 |archive-date=27 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200727123245/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-borussia-dortmund-live-line-ups-stats-james-rodriguez-lewandowski-3439 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=6 April 2019 |title=Robert Lewandowski scores his 200th Bundesliga goal, against Borussia Dortmund! |publisher=Bundesliga |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/news/Bundesliga/robert-lewandowski-scores-200th-bundesliga-goal-bayern-munich-borussia-dortmund-3525 |access-date=7 April 2019 |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806200716/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-scores-200th-bundesliga-goal-bayern-munich-borussia-dortmund-3525 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Lewandowski ended the [[2018–19 Bundesliga|league campaign]] as the Bundesliga's top goalscorer with 22 goals for the fourth time.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Bayern Munich's Robert Lewandowski claims Bundesliga topscorer prize for fourth time |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-s-robert-lewandowski-claims-bundesliga-topscorer-prize-for-fourth-time-4597 |access-date=18 May 2019 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=27 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200727115858/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-s-robert-lewandowski-claims-bundesliga-topscorer-prize-for-fourth-time-4597 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 25 May, he scored a brace as Bayern won against [[RB Leipzig|Leipzig]] 3–0 in the [[2019 DFB-Pokal Final]]. With his goals, he became the all-time top scorer in the [[DFB-Pokal]] finals with six, surpassing Gerd Müller on five.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Robert Lewandowski hits brace as Bayern Munich beat RB Leipzig in DFB Cup final to seal the double |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/rb-leipzig-bayern-munich-live-dfb-cup-final-james-rodriguez-lewandowski-neuer-4678 |access-date=25 May 2019 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=25 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190525223235/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/rb-leipzig-bayern-munich-live-dfb-cup-final-james-rodriguez-lewandowski-neuer-4678 |url-status=live }}</ref> Lewandowski finished the [[2018–19 FC Bayern Munich season|season]] with 40 goals in 47 matches in all competitions, reaching the 40-goal landmark for the fourth consecutive season, also winning his second domestic double with Bayern.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 May 2019 |title=Bayern Munich win German Cup, secure domestic double |url=https://www.dw.com/en/bayern-munich-win-german-cup-secure-domestic-double/a-48875488 |access-date=27 May 2019 |publisher=Deutsche Welle |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806200204/https://www.dw.com/en/bayern-munich-win-german-cup-secure-domestic-double/a-48875488 |url-status=live }}</ref>

====2019–2020: Treble, Best FIFA Men's Player, and UEFA Men's Player of the Year====
[[File:2019147200143 2019-05-27 Fussball 1.FC Kaiserslautern vs FC Bayern München - Sven - 1D X MK II - 2462 - B70I0762 (cropped) (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|Lewandowski playing for [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in 2019]]

On 12 August, Lewandowski scored his first goal of the [[2019–20 FC Bayern Munich season|season]] when Bayern defeated [[FC Energie Cottbus|Energie Cottbus]] 3–1 in the [[2019–20 DFB-Pokal#First round|first round of the DFB-Pokal]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=FC Bayern vs Energie Cottbus Match Report |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/matches/profis/dfb-pokal/2019-2020/fc-energie-cottbus-fc-bayern-muenchen-10-08-2019-matchreport |access-date=20 August 2019 |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |archive-date=7 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200807024324/https://fcbayern.com/en/matches/profis/dfb-pokal/2019-2020/fc-energie-cottbus-fc-bayern-muenchen-10-08-2019-matchreport |url-status=live }}</ref> Four days later, he scored two goals in the [[2019–20 Bundesliga]] opener against Hertha BSC. With his goals, Lewandowski set a Bundesliga record for scoring a goal in the season opener for the fifth year in a row.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lewandowski sets record |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2019/08/lewandowski-sets-record |access-date=20 August 2019 |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806221331/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2019/08/lewandowski-sets-record |url-status=live }}</ref> He then scored a hat-trick against Schalke at the [[Veltins Arena]] on 24 August, as the Reds won 3–0.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lewy the hero as Reds give Schalke the blues |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/matchreports/2019/08/match-report-bundesliga-md2-fc-schalke-04-v-fc-bayern-240819 |access-date=25 August 2019 |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806224119/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/matchreports/2019/08/match-report-bundesliga-md2-fc-schalke-04-v-fc-bayern-240819 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 29 August, Lewandowski extended his contract at Bayern until 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Robert Lewandowski extends stay at FC Bayern through 2023 |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2019/08/robert-lewandowski-extends-stay-at-fc-bayern-through-2023 |access-date=29 August 2019 |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806215821/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2019/08/robert-lewandowski-extends-stay-at-fc-bayern-through-2023 |url-status=live }}</ref> Lewandowski scored his 200th goal for Bayern in a 3–0 win against Serbian club [[Red Star Belgrade]] in the [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] on 18 September.<ref>{{Cite web |title=FC Bayern send 'signal to other teams' |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2019/09/fc-bayern-send-signal-to-other-teams-champions-league-red-star-belgrade |access-date=23 September 2019 |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806215813/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2019/09/fc-bayern-send-signal-to-other-teams-champions-league-red-star-belgrade |url-status=live }}</ref> Later that month, after scoring his tenth goal of the campaign during a 3–2 win over Paderborn, he became the first player in Bundesliga history to achieve double figures for goals scored after the first six match rounds.<ref>{{Cite news |date=28 September 2019 |title=Philippe Coutinho stars and Robert Lewandowski strikes again as Bayern Munich dispatch spirited Paderborn |publisher=Bundesliga |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/paderborn-bayern-munich-live-line-ups-stats-robert-lewandowski-coutinho-neuer-7179 |access-date=30 September 2019 |archive-date=31 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200731200624/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/paderborn-bayern-munich-live-line-ups-stats-robert-lewandowski-coutinho-neuer-7179 |url-status=live }}</ref> Lewandowski then became the first player in Bundesliga history to score in each of the opening nine, ten and eleven matches of a season, surpassing the record of eight set by former Borussia Dortmund striker [[Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=26 October 2019 |title=Bayern Munich's Robert Lewandowski breaks Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang's Bundesliga record |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-overtakes-aubameyang-scores-in-record-9th-game-bayern-7753 |access-date=26 October 2019 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806164042/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-overtakes-aubameyang-scores-in-record-9th-game-bayern-7753 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=3 November 2019 |title=Robert Lewandowski strike scant consolation as Eintracht Frankfurt punish ten-man Bayern Munich |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/eintracht-frankfurt-bayern-munich-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-coutinho-7847 |access-date=3 November 2019 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=27 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200727100053/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/eintracht-frankfurt-bayern-munich-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-coutinho-7847 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=9 November 2019 |title=Robert Lewandowski at the double as Hansi Flick's Bayern Munich humble Borussia Dortmund in Der Klassiker |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-borussia-dortmund-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-reus-coutinho-8001 |access-date=10 November 2019 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=27 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200727093905/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-borussia-dortmund-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-reus-coutinho-8001 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 26 November, Lewandowski scored 4 goals in under 15 minutes as Bayern defeated Red Star Belgrade 6–0 in their reverse fixture and clinched first place in their [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League group stage#Group B|Champions League group]], setting a new record for fastest time to score four goals in a Champions League match. He also became only the second player ever to score four goals in multiple Champions League matches.<ref>{{Cite web |date=27 November 2019 |title=Robert Lewandowski makes Champions League history as Bayern Munich hammers Red Star |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2019/11/26/football/robert-lewandowski-bayern-munich-champions-league-red-star-spt-intl/index.html |access-date=29 November 2019 |publisher=CNN |archive-date=6 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806174944/https://edition.cnn.com/2019/11/26/football/robert-lewandowski-bayern-munich-champions-league-red-star-spt-intl/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Whitebloom |first=Grey |date=3 December 2020 |title=Every Player to Have Scored Four Champions League Goals in a Single Game |url=https://www.90min.com/posts/every-player-to-score-four-champions-league-goals-in-one-game |access-date=24 January 2024 |website=90min.com |language=en-GB |archive-date=24 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240124194129/https://www.90min.com/posts/every-player-to-score-four-champions-league-goals-in-one-game |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 25 February 2020, Lewandowski equalled [[Cristiano Ronaldo]]'s record of nine away goals in a season in Europe's top club competition. He did so by scoring a goal in a 3–0 win against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]] (he also assisted [[Serge Gnabry]] twice in that match).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lewandowski matches Ronaldo scoring feat as Bayern smash Chelsea at Stamford Bridge |url=https://www.goal.com/en-in/news/lewandowski-matches-ronaldo-scoring-feat-as-bayern-smash-chelsea-/3akpnze6z7au1hxlb06a0hvfo |access-date=26 February 2020 |website=Goal |publisher=DAZN Group |location=Leeds |archive-date=18 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200818091213/https://www.goal.com/en-in/news/lewandowski-matches-ronaldo-scoring-feat-as-bayern-smash-chelsea-/3akpnze6z7au1hxlb06a0hvfo |url-status=live }}</ref> On 10 August, Lewandowski scored a brace and provided two assists in a 4–1 win over Chelsea in the [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Round of 16|return leg]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 August 2020 |title=Robert Lewandowski fires Bayern Munich past Chelsea and into UEFA Champions League quarter-finals |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-chelsea-champions-league-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-pulisic-12316 |access-date=17 August 2020 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=22 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200822211547/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-chelsea-champions-league-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-pulisic-12316 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 14 August, he assisted and scored in [[FC Barcelona 2–8 FC Bayern Munich|Bayern's 8–2 decimation of Barcelona]] in the [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Quarter-finals|quarter-finals]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Marsh |first=Charlotte |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/barcelona-vs-bay-munich/426065 |title=Barcelona 2–8 Bayern Munich: Bayern decimate Barca to reach Champions League semi-finals |date=15 August 2020 |access-date=16 June 2021 |work=Sky Sports |archive-date=24 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210624201024/https://www.skysports.com/football/barcelona-vs-bay-munich/426065 |url-status=live }}</ref> Lewandowski scored another goal, to be 15 goals in total, in his ninth consecutive Champions League match in Bavarian's [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Semi-finals|semifinal]] 3–0 win against [[Olympique Lyonnais|Lyon]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Smith |first=Rory |date=19 August 2020 |title=Bayern Munich Beats Lyon and Will Face P.S.G. in Champions League Final |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/19/sports/soccer/bayern-munich-lyon-live-score.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200819152035/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/19/sports/soccer/bayern-munich-lyon-live-score.html |archive-date=19 August 2020 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |access-date=23 August 2020 |work=The New York Times}}</ref> His European scoring streak ended when he failed to score a goal in the [[2020 UEFA Champions League Final|Champions League final match]] against [[Paris Saint-Germain F.C.|Paris Saint-Germain]] on 23 August; nevertheless, Bayern defeated PSG 1–0, giving Lewandowski his first Champions League title.<ref>{{Cite web |date=23 August 2020 |title=Bayern Munich beat Paris Saint-Germain to win Champions League |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report?gameId=573698 |access-date=23 August 2020 |publisher=ESPN |archive-date=31 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200831175653/https://www.espn.com/soccer/report?gameId=573698 |url-status=live }}</ref> He also became the second player ever to win the European [[Treble (association football)|treble]] while being the top scorer in all three competitions, repeating [[Johan Cruyff]]'s achievement with [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] from the [[1971-72 AFC Ajax season|1971–72 season]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://us.marca.com/claro/futbol/champions-league/2020/08/22/5f411a2b22601da0078b45e7.html |title=El récord de Cruyff que podría alcanzar Lewandowski de ganar la Champions |date=22 August 2020 |access-date=16 June 2021 |work=Marca |language=es |trans-title=Cruyff's record that Lewandowski could reach to win the Champions League |archive-date=21 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211021052643/https://us.marca.com/claro/futbol/champions-league/2020/08/22/5f411a2b22601da0078b45e7.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> However, Lewandowski was the first to do so as the sole top scorer in all three competitions.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sobczak |first=Krzysztof |date=24 August 2020 |title=Podwójna potrójna korona Lewandowskiego. Polak dołączył do Johana Cruyffa |trans-title=Lewandowski's double triple crown. Pole joined Johan Cruyff |url=https://sport.radiozet.pl/Pilka-nozna/Liga-Mistrzow/Podwojna-potrojna-korona-Roberta-Lewandowskiego.-Polak-jak-Johan-Cruyff |access-date=6 September 2020 |website=Radio Zet |language=pl |archive-date=26 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210126162743/https://sport.radiozet.pl/Pilka-nozna/Liga-Mistrzow/Podwojna-potrojna-korona-Roberta-Lewandowskiego.-Polak-jak-Johan-Cruyff |url-status=live }}</ref>

====2020–2021: Ballon d'Or Striker of the Year and European Golden Shoe====
[[File:Bayern Munchen players.jpg|thumb|right|Lewandowski (second from right) with his [[Bayern Munich]] teammates during a Champions League match against [[Lokomotiv Moscow]] in 2020]]
On 18 September, in an 8–0 win over [[FC Schalke 04|Schalke 04]], in which Lewandowski scored a penalty, he provided a ''[[rabona]]'' assist to [[Thomas Müller]], which was praised as the best of the season.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Bundesliga &#124; Video: Die Top-10-Assists der Saison 2020/21 |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/de/bundesliga/news/video-top-10-assists-vorlage-rabona-lewandowski-hacke-lupfer-2020-21-16298 |access-date=21 November 2023 |website=bundesliga.com |archive-date=21 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231121033009/https://www.bundesliga.com/de/bundesliga/news/video-top-10-assists-vorlage-rabona-lewandowski-hacke-lupfer-2020-21-16298 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=4 July 2021 |title=BAYERN'S TOP 5 ASSISTS IN THE 2020/21 BUNDESLIGA SEASON |url=https://fcbayern.com/fcbayerntv/en/videos/2021/01/07/bayerns-top-5-assists-in-the-2020-21-bundesliga-season |access-date=21 November 2023 |publisher=FC Bayern |archive-date=21 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231121031118/https://fcbayern.com/fcbayerntv/en/videos/2021/01/07/bayerns-top-5-assists-in-the-2020-21-bundesliga-season |url-status=live }}</ref> On 24 September, Lewandowski assisted [[Leon Goretzka]]'s opener in 2–1 victory over [[2019–20 UEFA Europa League]] winners [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]] in the [[2020 UEFA Super Cup]] in [[Budapest]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=24 September 2020 |title=Super Cup: Bayern Munich 2–1 Sevilla (AET) |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/54270055 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=25 April 2021 |archive-date=25 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200925114844/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/54270055 |url-status=live }}</ref> 6 days later, he played in Bayern's 3–2 win over Borussia Dortmund in the [[2020 DFL-Supercup]], to win their fifth trophy of the year.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Skilbeck |first=John |date=1 October 2020 |title=Bayern Munich 3–2 Borussia Dortmund: Kimmich delivers Supercup for Flick's men |url=https://www.goal.com/en-in/match/bayern-m%C3%BCnchen-v-borussia-dortmund/8p8dc1rcs422ezm6ign3kyvx0 |website=Goal |access-date=25 April 2021 |archive-date=24 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210424194443/https://www.goal.com/en-in/match/bayern-m%C3%BCnchen-v-borussia-dortmund/8p8dc1rcs422ezm6ign3kyvx0 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 4 October, he scored all four goals in a 4–3 win against Hertha BSC.<ref>{{Cite web |date=4 October 2020 |title=Robert Lewandowski scores four as Bayern Munich edge Hertha Berlin |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-hertha-berlin-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-davies-cunha-13075 |access-date=4 October 2020 |website=Bundesliga.com |archive-date=18 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210118155005/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-hertha-berlin-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-davies-cunha-13075 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 24 October, he scored a hat-trick in a 5–0 win against Eintracht Frankfurt, to become the first player in Bundesliga to score ten goals in only five matches.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 October 2020 |title=Lewandowski sets new Bundesliga goal record as Bayern Munich thrash Eintracht Frankfurt |url=https://www.goal.com/en-in/news/lewandowski-sets-new-bundesliga-goal-record-as-bayern-munich/luwxu4p2hur51iceo14k7a138 |website=Goal.com |access-date=24 October 2020 |archive-date=28 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028031112/https://www.goal.com/en-in/news/lewandowski-sets-new-bundesliga-goal-record-as-bayern-munich/luwxu4p2hur51iceo14k7a138 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 16 December, he scored a brace against Wolfsburg to be the third player to pass the 250-goal mark in Bundesliga, after Gerd Müller and [[Klaus Fischer]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 December 2020 |title=Bayern Munich 2–1 VfL Wolfsburg |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/55289392 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=23 December 2020 |archive-date=17 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201217021612/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/55289392 |url-status=live }}</ref> After winning the treble with Bayern Munich and his performances in the tournaments,<ref>{{Cite web |date=12 December 2020 |title=The Best FIFA Football Awards™ – News – Robert Lewandowski: A natural-born finisher |url=https://www.fifa.com/the-best-fifa-football-awards/news/robert-lewandowski-a-natural-born-finisher |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201212194656/https://www.fifa.com/the-best-fifa-football-awards/news/robert-lewandowski-a-natural-born-finisher |url-status=dead |archive-date=12 December 2020 |access-date=17 December 2020 |publisher=FIFA}}</ref> he was named [[The Best FIFA Men's Player]] [[The Best FIFA Football Awards 2020|2020]] on 17 December, becoming the first Polish player to win the award.<ref>{{cite web |first=Ben |last=Church |date=17 December 2020 |title=Lewandowski and Bronze named players of the year at Best FIFA Football Awards |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2020/12/17/football/robert-lewandowski-lucy-bronze-fifa-best-award-spt-intl/index.html |access-date=17 December 2020 |publisher=CNN |archive-date=9 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210109011654/https://edition.cnn.com/2020/12/17/football/robert-lewandowski-lucy-bronze-fifa-best-award-spt-intl/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The cancellation of the 2020 [[Ballon d'Or]] was met with extensive criticism, as most news and sports organisations believed Lewandowski was the front-runner and should have won the award.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Why Robert Lewandowski deserved this year's Best FIFA Men's Player award |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/why-lewandowski-deserves-to-win-fifa-best-award-no-ballon-d-or-ronaldo-messi-11926 |access-date=23 May 2021 |website=bundesliga.com – the official Bundesliga website |archive-date=17 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210517095626/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/why-lewandowski-deserves-to-win-fifa-best-award-no-ballon-d-or-ronaldo-messi-11926 |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 17 January 2021, Lewandowski became the first player in Bundesliga history to score 21 goals after just 16 games – a new Hinrunde record, beating Gerd Müller's 20 goals during the [[1968–69 Bundesliga|1968–69 season]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 January 2021 |title=Lewandowski breaks Muller's Bundesliga goals record after opening scoring for Bayern against Freiburg |url=https://www.goal.com/en/news/lewandowski-breaks-mullers-bundesliga-goals-record-after-opening-/jeace8r7l25217syw8o6imap4 |access-date=17 January 2021 |website=Goal |archive-date=22 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210122052510/https://www.goal.com/en/news/lewandowski-breaks-mullers-bundesliga-goals-record-after-opening-/jeace8r7l25217syw8o6imap4 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 8 February, he scored a brace in a 2–0 win over [[Al Ahly SC|Al Ahly]] in the [[2020 FIFA Club World Cup]] [[2020 FIFA Club World Cup#Semi-finals|semi-finals]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 February 2021 |title=Lewandowski brace books Bayern final place |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/lewandowski-brace-books-bayern-final-place#al-ahly-sc-v-fc-bayern-muenchen-fifa-club-world-cup-qatar-2020-x9035 |access-date=23 February 2021 |publisher=FIFA |archive-date=11 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210211151451/https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/lewandowski-brace-books-bayern-final-place#al-ahly-sc-v-fc-bayern-muenchen-fifa-club-world-cup-qatar-2020-x9035 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 11 February, he won the FIFA Club World Cup 2020 with the club after 1–0 win against [[Liga MX|Mexican top-flight club]] [[Tigres UANL|Tigres]] in the [[2020 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]], as Bayern became the second club ever (after Barcelona in [[2008–09 FC Barcelona season|2009]]) to win the [[Sextuple (football)|sextuple]]. He was also involved in [[Benjamin Pavard]]'s winning goal, and was named player of the tournament.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pavard completes sextuple for dominant Bayern |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/matches/match/400154013/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210211214953/https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/matches/match/400154013/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=11 February 2021 |access-date=9 March 2021 |publisher=FIFA}}</ref> On 23 February, Lewandowski opened the score in a 4–1 win against [[S.S. Lazio|Lazio]] in the first leg of the [[2020–21 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Round of 16|Champions League round of 16]], reaching his 72nd Champions League goal and surpassing [[Raúl (footballer)|Raúl]] as the [[List of UEFA Champions League top scorers|third highest goalscorer]] in the competition's history.<ref>{{Cite web |date=23 February 2021 |title=Lewandowski overtakes Raul as Champions League's third-highest scorer with only Ronaldo and Messi ahead |url=https://www.goal.com/en-us/news/lewandowski-overtakes-raul-champions-league-scorers/f0fb8pao8rpu1xwottt0w1ip6 |access-date=23 February 2021 |website=Goal.com |archive-date=23 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210523205323/https://www.goal.com/en-us/news/lewandowski-overtakes-raul-champions-league-scorers/f0fb8pao8rpu1xwottt0w1ip6 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 6 March, he scored his 12th Bundesliga hat-trick in a 4–2 win over his former club Borussia Dortmund, to reach 31 goals in 23 matches.<ref>{{Cite web |date=7 March 2021 |title=Record-smashing Robert Lewandowski underscores place among greats in Bayern Munich's Klassiker triumph |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/record-smashing-robert-lewandowski-underscores-bayern-munich-place-among-greats-15036 |website=Bundesliga |access-date=7 March 2021 |archive-date=23 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210523203820/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/record-smashing-robert-lewandowski-underscores-bayern-munich-place-among-greats-15036 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 13 March, he scored a goal in a 3–1 away win over Werder Bremen, hence he became the joint-second on the all-time Bundesliga scoring list with 268 goals along with Klaus Fischer.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 March 2021 |title=Robert Lewandowski equals Klaus Fischer on all-time top scorers list |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-overtakes-klaus-fischer-on-all-time-top-scorers-list-15108 |access-date=13 March 2021 |website=Bundesliga |archive-date=23 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210523203825/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-overtakes-klaus-fischer-on-all-time-top-scorers-list-15108 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 20 March, he surpassed Fischer, as he scored a [[Hat-trick#Perfect hat-trick|perfect hat-trick]] in the first half of a 4–0 win over VfB Stuttgart.<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 March 2021 |title=5 facts on Lewandowski's hat-trick against Stuttgart |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2021/03/5-facts-on-lewandowskis-hat-trick-against-stuttgart |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |access-date=24 March 2021 |archive-date=18 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210518154432/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2021/03/5-facts-on-lewandowskis-hat-trick-against-stuttgart |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 28 March, Lewandoski scored two goals in a 3–0 home win against [[Andorra national football team|Andorra]] in a [[2022 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group I|World Cup qualification]] match, and also damaged [[ligament]]s in his right knee (he was taken off after 63 minutes); he missed both [[2020–21 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Quarter-finals|Champions League quarter-final]] matches against Paris Saint-Germain, in which Bayern Munich lost on [[away goals rule]] after a 3–3 draw on aggregate.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 April 2021 |title=Robert Lewandowski returns to Bayern Munich training following knee injury |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-back-in-training-after-knee-injury-bayern-munich-15418 |website=Bundesliga |access-date=13 April 2021 |archive-date=15 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210415003240/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-back-in-training-after-knee-injury-bayern-munich-15418 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 24 April, he returned after almost a month on the sidelines in a 1–2 defeat to Mainz, where he scored in added time.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 April 2021 |title=Brilliant Mainz make Bayern Munich wait for Bundesliga title |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/mainz-bayern-munich-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-davies-sane-muller-neuer-15574 |website=Bundesliga |access-date=3 May 2021 |archive-date=15 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515210621/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/mainz-bayern-munich-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-davies-sane-muller-neuer-15574 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 8 May, he scored his 14th Bundesliga hat-trick in a 6–0 win over Borussia Mönchengladbach.<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 May 2021 |title=Robert Lewandowski closes in on history as Bayern Munich celebrate title by thrashing Borussia Mönchengladbach |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-borussia-monchengladbach-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-sane-15728 |website=Bundesliga |access-date=8 May 2021 |archive-date=17 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210517212004/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-borussia-monchengladbach-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-sane-15728 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 22 May, he broke Gerd Müller's record of 40 goals in the [[1971–72 Bundesliga|1971–72 season]] with a 90th-minute goal in Bayern's 5–2 win over Augsburg to reach his 41st goal on the [[2020–21 Bundesliga|final day of the season]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Robert Lewandowski sets new 41-goal league record for Bayern Munich |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-breaks-gerd-muller-40-goal-record-bayern-munich-15979 |access-date=22 May 2021 |publisher=Bundesliga |archive-date=23 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210523154853/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-breaks-gerd-muller-40-goal-record-bayern-munich-15979 |url-status=live }}</ref> He also managed to win his first [[European Golden Shoe]] award.<ref>{{Cite web |date=23 May 2021 |title=Robert Lewandowski beats Cristiano Ronaldo, Lionel Messi to European Golden Shoe |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/37617238/bayern-munich-robert-lewandowski-breaks-gerd-muller-bundesliga-goals-record |publisher=ESPN |access-date=22 May 2021 |archive-date=23 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210523000430/https://www.espn.com/soccer/bayern-munich-gerbayern_munich/story/4385575/bayern-munichs-robert-lewandowski-breaks-gerd-mullers-bundesliga-goals-record |url-status=live }}</ref> Lewandowski finished the [[2020–21 FC Bayern Munich season|season]] with 48 goals in 40 matches in all competitions, reaching at least the 40-goal landmark for the sixth time.<ref name=fcbayern>{{cite web |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2021/07/fans-vote-lewandowski-fc-bayern-2020-21-player-of-the-season |title=Fans vote Lewandowski FC Bayern 2020/21 Player of the Season |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=3 July 2021 |publisher=[[FC Bayern Munich]] |access-date=20 August 2021 |archive-date=20 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210820134200/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2021/07/fans-vote-lewandowski-fc-bayern-2020-21-player-of-the-season |url-status=live }}</ref>

====2021–2022: Final season with Bayern and second European Golden Shoe====
Lewandowski kicked off his [[2021–22 Bundesliga|2021–22 Bundesliga season]] with a [[Volley (association football)|volley-shot]] [[Equaliser (sports)|equaliser]] in a 1–1 opening fixture draw against Borussia Mönchengladbach on 13 August, making him the first player to score in seven consecutive Bundesliga opening games.<ref>{{cite news |author=<!--Staff writer(s)/no by-line.--> |title=Borussia Monchengladbach 1-1 Bayern Munich: Robert Lewandowski scores again |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/58206961 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |location=[[City of Salford|Salford]] |date=13 August 2021 |access-date=13 August 2021 |archive-date=13 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210813205117/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/58206961 |url-status=live }}</ref> He also scored a brace and [[backheel]]ed to Thomas Müller in a 3–1 away win against Dortmund in the [[2021 DFL-Supercup]] on 17 August. The match was preceded by a [[moment of silence]] for Gerd Müller, who died two days earlier.<ref>{{cite news |author=<!--Staff writer(s)/no by-line.--> |title=Borussia Dortmund 1-3 Bayern Munich: Robert Lewandowski double earns German Super Cup |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11899/12384159/borussia-dortmund-1-3-bayern-munich-robert-lewandowski-double-earns-german-super-cup |work=[[Sky Sports]] |location=London |publisher=[[Sky Group]] |date=18 August 2021 |access-date=20 August 2021 |archive-date=18 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210818072917/https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11899/12384159/borussia-dortmund-1-3-bayern-munich-robert-lewandowski-double-earns-german-super-cup |url-status=live }}</ref> On 28 August, he scored his 15th Bundesliga hat-trick in a 5–0 win over Hertha Berlin,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-hertha-berlin-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-davies-sane-17060 |title=Robert Lewandowski hits a hat-trick as Bayern Munich hammer Hertha Berlin |work=Bundesliga |date=28 August 2021 |access-date=28 August 2021 |archive-date=8 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210908140741/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/bayern-munich-hertha-berlin-live-line-ups-stats-lewandowski-davies-sane-17060 |url-status=live }}</ref> setting a new club and German record for the most consecutive appearances in all competitions with a goal at 16,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/matches/profis/bundesliga/2021-2022/fc-bayern-muenchen-hertha-bsc-28-08-2021-matchreport |title=Impressive Reds down Hertha |work=FC Bayern Munich |date=28 August 2021 |access-date=28 August 2021 |archive-date=28 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210828192323/https://fcbayern.com/en/matches/profis/bundesliga/2021-2022/fc-bayern-muenchen-hertha-bsc-28-08-2021-matchreport |url-status=live }}</ref> surpassing the previous record of 15 held by Gerd Müller from [[1969–70 FC Bayern Munich season|1969 to 1970]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-sets-goals-consecutive-games-bayern-munich-record-muller-17105 |title=Robert Lewandowski sets new Bayern Munich goals record |work=Bundesliga |date=28 August 2021 |access-date=28 August 2021 |archive-date=8 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210908100703/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-sets-goals-consecutive-games-bayern-munich-record-muller-17105 |url-status=live }}</ref> In addition, he managed to reach more than 300 goals with Bayern Munich in all competitions.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.goal.com/en/news/lewandowski-scores-300th-goal-for-bayern-as-bundesliga-champions-/1p2hkyqlxy8or1jwf86sygj1qy |title=Lewandowski scores 300th goal for Bayern as Bundesliga champions thrash Hertha Berlin |work=[[Goal (website)|Goal]] |date=28 August 2021 |access-date=28 August 2021 |archive-date=29 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210829112944/https://www.goal.com/en/news/lewandowski-scores-300th-goal-for-bayern-as-bundesliga-champions-/1p2hkyqlxy8or1jwf86sygj1qy |url-status=live }}</ref> On 18 September, Lewandowski scored in his 13th consecutive Bundesliga home match against [[VfL Bochum]], surpassing the previous league record of 12 held by Gerd Müller (October 1969 to April 1970) and [[Jupp Heynckes]] (June 1972 to February 1973).<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.sport1.de/news/fussball/bundesliga/2021/09/bundesliga-fc-bayern-robert-lewandowski-stellt-rekord-auf |title=Lewandowski übertrumpft Bayern-Legenden |trans-title=Lewandowski trumps Bayern legends |work=[[Sport1 (Germany)|Sport1]] |date=18 September 2021 |access-date=18 September 2021 |language=de |archive-date=18 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210918154554/https://www.sport1.de/news/fussball/bundesliga/2021/09/bundesliga-fc-bayern-robert-lewandowski-stellt-rekord-auf |url-status=live }}</ref> He ended the streak for most consecutive appearances in all competitions with a goal at 19, the new all-time German record.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 March 2022 |title=Robert Lewandowski's Bundesliga record collection |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-s-record-collection-bayern-munich-7764 |access-date=25 January 2024 |website=bundesliga.com |archive-date=29 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240229212302/https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/robert-lewandowski-s-record-collection-bayern-munich-7764 |url-status=live }}</ref>

